[observation.6.11.149]
task = "6.11"
file = "tests/support/lxgb_metadata.rs agent/crates/layerx-agentd/tests/support/real_authority.rs platform/hosted/core/tests/boundary.rs platform/hosted/authority/tests/real_node.rs"
symbol = "cargo fmt --all --check cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets"
observed = "Run from agent, cargo fmt --all --check exits 1 with 659 hunks over 90 files: 33 under agent, 15 under interop, 13 under platform and 598 under the vendored programs tree. Eighty-eight of those 90 files are byte-identical to 0717c87ca. The two that are not, platform/hosted/core/tests/boundary.rs and platform/hosted/authority/tests/real_node.rs, produce the same three and one hunks at 0717c87ca, at f89cb11c0 and at the integrated tip, and the exact unformatted lines of all three boundary.rs hunks are present in all three blobs, so no hunk originates in the integration. Checking either blob on its own with rustfmt --edition 2021 --check reports one entry more, for tests/support/lxgb_metadata.rs, which both files reach through the same path-attribute include and which the workspace run counts once in its own right. Run from agent, cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings exits 101 with one format_collect and three cast_possible_truncation diagnostics in tests/support/lxgb_metadata.rs, reached through the path-attribute include in agent/crates/layerx-agentd/tests/support/real_authority.rs while compiling the layerx-agentd budget_create and migration test targets. That helper, the include site, both test targets and the workspace clippy levels in agent/Cargo.toml are each byte-identical to 0717c87ca. Evidence: qual-logs/rebase-205-3.log and qual-logs/rebase-205-4.log."
assumption = "Both commands are reproducible from an unmodified checkout of 0717c87ca. Repairing them means reformatting vendored third-party sources and relaxing or restructuring a shared root test helper owned by another lane, so no formatter run, lint allowance, cast or assertion was introduced here."
severity = "blocker"

[observation.6.11.150]
task = "6.11"
file = "platform/hosted/node/bootstrap.sh contracts/config/checkpoint-settlement.json tests/daemon/program-admission.sh agent/crates/layerx-client/tests/availability_daemon.rs"
symbol = "genesis request has an unexpected length certificate_threshold GUARANTOR_ENTRIES"
observed = "cargo test --workspace run from agent exits 101 on the layerx-client availability_daemon target: real_daemon_availability_refusals drives tests/daemon/program-admission.sh, whose call to platform/hosted/node/bootstrap.sh refuses with the message that the genesis request has an unexpected length. bootstrap.sh compares the LXGB v2 request against the fixed size 395 plus the size of the genesis metadata, but it emits one guarantor record per certificate_threshold read from contracts/config/checkpoint-settlement.json, and each record is 81 bytes: a 32-byte sha256 identifier, a 33-byte compressed secp256k1 public key and a 16-byte zero field. The metadata term cancels on both sides, so with certificate_threshold set to 2 the request measures 851 bytes against an expected 770, an excess of exactly one guarantor record; the bound therefore describes a single-guarantor request only. All four files are byte-identical to 0717c87ca. The same test additionally requires build/tests/lxp_test_program_admission and build/tests/lxp_test_daemon_finality_authority, which the make test target does not build, so they must be made before the workspace test run. Evidence: qual-logs/rebase-205-5.log and qual-logs/availability-harness.log."
assumption = "The mismatch is arithmetic and independent of the metadata length. Correcting it means changing either the length bound in the node bootstrap or the default certificate threshold in the settlement document, both owned by other lanes, so neither the bound nor the threshold was altered here."
severity = "blocker"

[observation.6.11.151]
task = "6.11"
file = "agent/crates/layerx-crypto/src/disclosure.rs agent/crates/layerx-crypto/tests/disclosure.rs include/layerx/lx_asset.h"
symbol = "decoded_fields native_withdrawal_refuses_old_unknown_truncated_extended_and_invalid_fields"
observed = "cargo test --workspace run from agent exits 101 on the layerx-crypto disclosure target. The assertion at agent/crates/layerx-crypto/tests/disclosure.rs:252 requires that binding an activity of asset ordinal 10 returns UnsupportedActivity. That test arrives only from 0717c87ca, where the disclosure layer decodes asset ordinals 5 and 6 and asset ordinal 9 through the withdraw path, so ordinal 10 is refused. The predicate in decoded_fields that routes asset ordinals 1, 4, 6, 7, 8, 10 and 11 to payment_fields arrives only from f89cb11c0, introduced by 58e5656a8, and makes ordinals 10 and 11 disclosable. include/layerx/lx_asset.h defines LX_ASSET_MINT as ordinal 10 and LX_ASSET_BURN as ordinal 11 identically on both sides, so no ordinal was renumbered and the two sides did not collide over a value. The integrated decoded_fields retains both dispatches in the correct order: the withdraw early return for ordinal 9 precedes the payment predicate, and the ten remaining disclosure tests pass. The single failing assertion is the only point at which the two behaviours are mutually exclusive: it holds exactly when mint disclosure is absent. Evidence: qual-logs/rebase-205-5.log and qual-logs/rebase-205-5-nofailfast.log."
assumption = "Mint and burn disclosure is the intended behaviour, so the payment predicate stands unchanged and the negative example could not remain at ordinal 10. The example moved to asset ordinal 12, which include/layerx/lx_asset.h leaves undefined above burn and which neither the withdraw early return, the payment predicate nor the send and receive semantics route, so the property that an activity the disclosure layer does not decode is refused as an unsupported activity is still asserted. No assertion was relaxed, no ordinal was removed from the predicate, every other case in the test is unchanged, and the layerx-crypto disclosure target passes all eleven tests."
severity = "blocker"

[observation.6.11.152]
task = "6.11"
file = "package-lock.json agent/sdk/typescript/package.json agent/sdk/typescript/package-lock.json platform/middleware/agent/package.json"
symbol = "npm ci --workspaces --include-workspace-root"
observed = "npm ci run in agent/sdk/typescript and npm ci --workspaces --include-workspace-root run from the repository root both exit 1 with EUSAGE, reporting ws@8.21.3 and @types/ws@8.18.1 missing from the lock file. agent/sdk/typescript is one of the nineteen workspaces declared in the root package.json, so npm resolves it against the root package-lock.json and ignores the package-lock.json that sits beside the SDK manifest. 368e9e928 added ws and @types/ws to the SDK manifest and to that ignored local lock only, and 050e61ae2 added @sidiora/layerx-seller-middleware to platform/middleware/agent without recording it either, so the root lock described neither dependency and every workspace install refused before any package was fetched. Evidence: qual-logs/rebase-205-10.log."
assumption = "Regenerating the root lock with npm install --package-lock-only adds the two ws entries and the missing workspace reference, and carries across the Apache-2.0 identifier that nine workspace manifests already declare where the lock still held the earlier SEE LICENSE IN LICENSE string; no resolved package version, integrity or URL moved. The regenerated lock is recorded in 4994801b2105049398bea454e11a547dc312968d and the workspace install then completes."
severity = "blocker"

