file = "human/Cargo.lock Makefile platform/hosted/human/test_provision.py"
symbol = "layerx-human-identity-provider human-test-hosted-provisioning identity_provider_binary"
observed = "python3 -m unittest discover -s platform/hosted/human -p 'test_*.py' ran 31 tests and exited 1 at revision f52a5937890a (parent 88f0268fbe6b) with one failure and one error, both requiring the real provider binary human/target/debug/layerx-human-identity-provider: test_provision.RegistrationInputTests.test_real_owner_output_is_not_a_protocol_registration and test_provision.RegistrationInputTests.test_generated_catalog_uses_real_keys_and_production_registry_modules; qual-logs/c6/gate-13.log. At that revision the binary could not be produced: cargo build --locked --manifest-path human/Cargo.toml -p layerx-human-identity-provider exited 101 because human/Cargo.lock needed updating, and offline resolution failed on data-encoding, pinned =2.9.0 by layerx-human-security-provider against ^2 required by tungstenite =0.30.0 through layerx-sdk, which layerx-explorer-index depends on by path; qual-logs/c6/gate-20.log. Main has since regenerated human/Cargo.lock, which carries data-encoding 2.9.0 and tungstenite 0.30.0 together and on the rebased head passes cargo metadata --manifest-path human/Cargo.toml --offline --locked with exit 0 (a resolution check, not a build), added the target human-test-hosted-provisioning (Makefile:2073) that builds the provider (Makefile:2077) before running pytest over platform/hosted/human (Makefile:2080) as a prerequisite of human-test (Makefile:2064), and test_provision.py resolves the binary through LAYERX_HUMAN_IDENTITY_PROVIDER_BIN (platform/hosted/human/test_provision.py:11, platform/hosted/human/test_provision.py:16). The other 29 tests passed at the gate revision, including all four in test_material.py and the three added by this branch; discovery of test_guardians.py and test_owner_request.py needs pytest, supplied read-only through PYTHONPATH in that run. The whole human tree is identical to main on this branch."
assumption = "The lock and dependency-edge cause recorded at the gate revision is gone from this revision. Neither the Human tests nor the provider build was re-run after the rebase, so no result for them on this revision is recorded here. No lock file was regenerated by this branch, no pin widened, and neither test was skipped, marked expected-failure or weakened."

[observation.6.8.6916]
task = "6.8"
severity = "suspect"
file = "cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_process.c src/modules/asset/lx_asset_registry.c platform/hosted/registry/tests/native_deployment.rs"
symbol = "collect_assets lx_asset_committed_records"
observed = "At revision f52a5937890a (parent 88f0268fbe6b) the three tests of the registry native_deployment target all stopped before their first assertion because the daemon they spawn refused to start: layerxd --serve exited early with exit status: 1: layerxd: bootstrap configuration failed with result -402, raised at platform/hosted/registry/tests/native_deployment.rs:374. The whole package run exited 101 with every other registry target green, 25 tests passed across the unit, isolation, deadline and journal targets and 3 failed, all three in native_deployment; qual-logs/c6/gate-8.log. The same three failures reproduced with the target run alone; qual-logs/c6/gate-9.log. -402 is LXP_ERR_ASSET_MISMATCH (include/layerx/lxp_result.h:77). On this revision the message is printed at cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_process.c:5375 with stage still configuration, the value it is given at cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_process.c:4955 and first replaced at cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_process.c:5149, so the refusal happens inside that window. The only LXP_ERR_ASSET_MISMATCH return reachable in it is collect_assets, called at cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_process.c:5102, which refuses when lx_asset_committed_records (src/modules/asset/lx_asset_registry.c:760) reports no committed asset record (cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_process.c:595) and when a registered account names an asset that is not among them (cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_process.c:602). The harness builds its own genesis with a freshly generated asset through layerx-genesis-build (platform/hosted/registry/tests/native_deployment.rs:434). Since the gate revision main has changed src/modules/asset/lx_asset_registry.c, the file that defines lx_asset_committed_records, and the harness now drives the producer through a treasury signer process (platform/hosted/registry/tests/native_deployment.rs:1318); the binaries this commit builds built clean at the gate revision, qual-logs/c6/gate-1.log, exit 0."
assumption = "Whether the genesis this harness produces and the daemon bootstrap that consumes it still disagree about committed asset records on this revision is not established: the target was not re-run after the rebase. This branch does not touch that path: the only C file it changes is cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_listener.c, its entire diff is inside the HTTP field-name match of request_parse, and that code cannot run before --serve has finished bootstrapping. Nothing was stubbed, no genesis was hand-written, no assertion was relaxed and neither the target nor any of its three tests was skipped or marked expected-failure."

[observation.6.8.6917]
task = "6.8"
severity = "note"
file = "platform/emulator/build.rs Makefile"
symbol = "main programs-build PROGRAMS_WORKSPACE_CARGO"
observed = "While this branch was being qualified at revision f52a5937890a (parent 88f0268fbe6b), a cargo command in the platform workspace that builds layerx-platform-emulator deadlocked against itself whenever CARGO_TARGET_DIR was exported, observed twice against one shared target directory: /proc/locks showed the outer cargo as the write holder of the build-directory lock, its own descendant cargo build queued directly behind it, and more than ten unrelated processes queued behind that, with neither side advancing. At that revision platform/emulator/build.rs ran make build with the inherited environment and programs-build ran the nested cargo through PROGRAMS_CARGO alone, so the nested cargo inherited CARGO_TARGET_DIR and waited on the flock only its own ancestor could release; the four platform-workspace gates recorded for this branch, qual-logs/c6/gate-7.log through qual-logs/c6/gate-10.log, were run with PROGRAMS_CARGO='env CARGO_TARGET_DIR=<worktree>/programs/target cargo' in their environment to clear it. Main has since given the nested build its own target directory: Makefile:3066-3067 define PROGRAMS_TARGET_DIR and PROGRAMS_WORKSPACE_CARGO next to PROGRAMS_CARGO ?= cargo (Makefile:3065), programs-build (Makefile:3081) and the rule for PROGRAMS_RUNTIME_LIB (Makefile:3078) run through PROGRAMS_WORKSPACE_CARGO, make build still reaches programs-build through build: layerx-guarantor (Makefile:3414) and the order-only prerequisite at Makefile:3418, and platform/emulator/build.rs:31-32 remove CARGO_TARGET_DIR and CARGO_BUILD_TARGET_DIR from the environment of the make it launches (platform/emulator/build.rs:28-34)."
assumption = "The deadlock recorded at the gate revision is addressed on this revision by main; no platform-workspace build was run after the rebase to exercise it, so no result on this revision is recorded here. Neither the Makefile nor the emulator build script is changed by this branch."

[observation.6.11.2300]
task = "6.11"
file = "include/layerx/lxp_ledger.h src/ledger/lx_account_registry.c src/state/lxp_state_root.c src/state/lxp_snapshot.c"
symbol = "lx_account_registry lx_account_index_entry lx_account_registry_reserve account_registry_root"
observed = "The account registry no longer stores accounts in a fixed inline array of 512. lx_account_registry is now a 72-byte handle: a pointer to a slot array, a pointer to a sorted index of {account id, slot} entries, a committed capacity and a count. Storage is a PROT_NONE MAP_NORESERVE reservation taken once and turned readable and writable page by page with mprotect as the count grows, doubling from LX_ACCOUNT_REGISTRY_INITIAL_SLOTS, which is 512. The reservation, not a realloc, is what makes growth safe: lx_account pointers are handed to callers and held across account creation, and a moving allocation would dangle every one of them. The index is maintained by binary-search insert and remove, so a lookup is O(log n) rather than the linear scan it replaced, and every consumer that needs accounts in canonical id order - the state-root leaves, the state-diff account list, the snapshot writer - reads that order from the index instead of sorting a copy of the slot array. The state root is unchanged by this: leaves_root already sorted the leaves by key, so enumerating in index order builds the same sorted leaf array that enumerating in slot order built. The evidence is direct: build/tests/lxp_test_state_proof --vectors on this change and on an unmodified checkout of main in a separate worktree produce byte-identical output, kept at qual-logs/baseline-state-proofs.json."
assumption = "The row's default permitted a consensus-visible state-root change on the disposable beta chain. None was needed and none was made, so no chain restart follows."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.11.2301]
task = "6.11"
file = "src/ledger/lx_account_registry.c src/protocol/lxp_module_ctx.c tests/programs/test_accounts.c"
symbol = "LX_ACCOUNT_REGISTRY_CAPACITY lx_account_registry_reserve lxp_ctx_asset_issuance_stage registry_growth"
observed = "The refusals that answered LXP_ERR_ARENA_EXHAUSTED once the registry held 512 accounts are gone from the staging paths in lxp_module_ctx.c; what remains there is LXP_MODULE_MAX_STAGED_ACCOUNTS, which bounds one activity's staged accounts and not the ledger. LX_ACCOUNT_REGISTRY_CAPACITY survives with the value 1048576, but it is no longer a policy cap: it is the size of the address-space reservation, 696254464 bytes of slots plus 41943040 bytes of index at sizeof(lx_account) 664 and sizeof(lx_account_index_entry) 40. Asking lx_account_registry_reserve for more slots than that answers LXP_ERR_LENGTH_LIMIT and failing to commit pages answers LXP_ERR_ARENA_EXHAUSTED; both are resource refusals raised where the resource is acquired, not admission decisions. Nothing between 512 and that ceiling refuses for any reason other than gas, fee or authority. tests/programs/test_accounts.c registry_growth opens 1543 module-value accounts, three times the initial commitment plus seven, and checks the count, the committed capacity, the index invariant, every id's lookup, the registry root, and one account proof in every 173 against that root with leaf_count equal to 1543."
assumption = "A ceiling that is a memory reservation rather than a rule is left in place and named as such. Raising it further is a question of how much address space one process may reserve and belongs to whoever sizes the node, not to this row."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.11.2302]
task = "6.11"
file = "src/state/lxp_journal.c src/state/lxp_state_diff.c src/replica/lxp_replay.c cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_process.c cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_lni.c cmd/layerx-genesis/lxp_genesis_builder.c cmd/layerx-guarantor/runtime.c src/modules/asset/lx_asset_execution.h src/modules/programs/deploy.c src/protocol/lxp_kernel.c"
symbol = "lxp_state_transition_apply lxp_state_snapshot_publish_guarded replay_batch lx_account_list_did asset_execute_typed"
observed = "Turning the inline array into a handle is not source compatible with four kinds of consumer, and each kind had to be repaired for the tree to be correct rather than merely to compile. First, buffers dimensioned from LX_ACCOUNT_REGISTRY_CAPACITY: lxp_journal.c held lxp_state_account_delta accounts[CAPACITY * 2]; lxp_kernel.c, programs/deploy.c, lxp_daemon_lni.c and lxp_daemon_evidence.c sized lx_account_list_did and proof buffers from it; the daemon and the guarantor dimensioned transfer-asset arrays with the account constant although they are indexed by asset. These are now sized from the live counts and, for the asset arrays, from LX_ASSET_REGISTRY_CAPACITY. Second, value copies used to capture a before state: struct assignment now copies pointers, so lxp_replay.c replay_batch, the daemon's base_accounts, lx_asset_execution.h's receive preview, tests/bridge/test_credit.c, tests/test_state_commitment_transition.c and tests/support/lxp_real_replay.h would each have compared a registry with itself and passed vacuously; they take lx_account_registry_copy, or the arena-backed lx_account_registry_borrow where the lifetime is an arena. Third, copies and wipes sized with sizeof(registry->accounts), which is now a pointer width: lxp_state_snapshot_publish_guarded copies count entries and the index instead, and the publication guard reserves the needed capacity while it holds the lock so the publish itself cannot fail; the three lxp_secure_zero(accounts, sizeof(*accounts)) sites release the mapping before wiping the 72-byte handle. Fourth, ownership: a registry now has a destructor, and lx_account_registry_release is called wherever one was previously abandoned, including per-activity previews and per-publication snapshots, which would otherwise exhaust vm.max_map_count."
assumption = "The row's owned paths named the registry, the state root, the snapshot and the module context. The consumers above are outside them, and the changes are the minimum that keeps their existing behaviour true under the new storage; each is listed with its reason in the lane's NEEDS.md."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.11.2303]
task = "6.11"
file = "src/state/lxp_journal.c src/protocol/lxp_genesis.c src/ledger/lx_account_registry.c"
symbol = "lxp_state_transition_apply lxp_genesis_materialize lx_account_registry_slot_insert lx_account_registry_slot_remove lx_account_registry_index_rebuild"
observed = "Two sites outside the registry inserted or removed accounts by writing the slot array directly, and a durable index makes both wrong. lxp_state_transition_apply appended at count and memmoved the tail down on a delete; it now goes through lx_account_registry_slot_insert and lx_account_registry_slot_remove, and reserves for all additions before it mutates anything so the loop cannot fail for memory once it has started. One behaviour there is deliberately stricter: a delta that deletes an account the store does not hold used to decrement the count and corrupt the registry silently and now answers LXP_FATAL_INVARIANT; the validation pass that runs immediately before already refused that case, so no accepted transition changes. lxp_genesis_materialize wrote each manifest account into accounts->accounts[index] and incremented count, which left the index empty, so lx_account_registry_index_validate refused inside lxp_state_root and lxp_genesis_state_root answered LXP_ERR_NON_CANONICAL; it now reserves manifest->account_count and inserts each materialized account through the same helper. The manifest accounts are already sorted by account id, so slot order is unchanged. lxp_genesis_state_root also owned a registry it never released and now releases it. lx_account_registry_index_rebuild, used by the snapshot restore path, sorts with qsort rather than the insertion sort it started as, because an insertion sort over a million-entry index is quadratic and would reintroduce the bound this row removes."
assumption = "The stricter refusal in the transition path is recorded rather than softened: the callers that could reach it are already guarded, and an unguarded caller should fail loudly rather than corrupt the registry."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.11.2304]
task = "6.11"
file = "tests/vectors/program_account_state_v2.vec programs/crates/layerx-programs-registry/src/account_state.rs programs/crates/layerx-programs-registry/tests/winddown.rs"
symbol = "max_program_accounts refused_program_accounts MAX_PROGRAM_VALUE_ACCOUNTS"
observed = "The shared vector program_account_state_v2.vec pinned max_program_accounts to 512 and refused_program_accounts to 513. That vector is asserted against LX_ACCOUNT_REGISTRY_CAPACITY by tests/programs/test_accounts.c shared_registration_boundaries and against MAX_PROGRAM_VALUE_ACCOUNTS by programs/crates/layerx-programs-registry/tests/winddown.rs, so the vector and both constants only move together. The vector now carries 1048576 and 1048577 and MAX_PROGRAM_VALUE_ACCOUNTS is 1_048_576. On the Rust side that constant bounds the length of an already decoded vector of bindings and of accounts in verify; it preallocates nothing, so raising it changes what a verifier accepts and not what it allocates. Had it stayed at 512, the Rust verifier would have refused a valid snapshot of a registry holding more than 512 accounts, reimposing at the client exactly the bound this row removes from the ledger."
assumption = "The Rust crate is outside the row's owned paths and is changed only to keep the shared vector consistent; the change is listed in the lane's NEEDS.md."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.11.2305]
task = "6.11"
file = "contracts/config/native-state-proofs.json tests/state/lxp_test_state_proof.c Makefile"
symbol = "test-state-proof native-state-proofs.json"
observed = "make CC=gcc test-state-proof fails on this revision: build/tests/lxp_test_state_proof --vectors and the checked-in contracts/config/native-state-proofs.json differ from byte 25. The failure is not caused by this change. An unmodified checkout of main was built in a separate worktree and its lxp_test_state_proof --vectors output is byte-identical to the output produced here, which is kept at qual-logs/baseline-state-proofs.json; both differ from the checked-in file in the same place. The differing bytes belong to a module-registration leaf of the universal subtree, built in universal_leaves from a registration's module id, abi version, enabled flag and activity-type count. Nothing in this change touches a module registration, and the account-tree leaf is identical in both files, so the account tree root is unchanged. The recorded vector is stale with respect to whatever last changed a module registration. test-state-proof is not part of make test, which passes here with exit 0."
assumption = "Regenerating contracts/config/native-state-proofs.json would rewrite a cross-language vector that the Solidity and Rust verifiers also read, and the change that made it stale is not this one, so the failure is recorded rather than repaired."
severity = "suspect"

[observation.6.11.2306]
task = "6.11"
file = "tests/test_genesis_manifest.c src/modules/asset/lx_asset_registry.c src/protocol/lxp_genesis.c"
symbol = "test-genesis-bootstrap check_version lxp_kernel_module_for_activity activity_types"
observed = "make CC=gcc test-genesis-bootstrap fails on this revision at tests/test_genesis_manifest.c:203, where check_version(LXP_PROTOCOL_VERSION_STATE_COMMITMENT) requires that asset activity ordinal 9, LX_ASSET_WITHDRAW, does not resolve through lxp_kernel_module_for_activity after the genesis module plan is registered. It resolves, because activity_types in src/modules/asset/lx_asset_registry.c registers all eleven asset activities including LX_ASSET_WITHDRAW. The failure is not caused by this change: an unmodified checkout of the same main revision built in a separate worktree fails at exactly the same line with the same message, recorded in qual-logs/baseline-genesis.log. The occupancy half of the same test, check_version(LXP_PROTOCOL_VERSION_OCCUPANCY), passes on the baseline and passes here, which is also the evidence that the genesis index-maintenance repair in observation 6.11.2303 restores parity rather than changing genesis output."
assumption = "Either the registration table or the test states the intended activity set for the state-commitment plan, and they disagree. Both are left exactly as written, because deciding which one is authoritative is a decision about the asset module's activity surface and not about account storage."
severity = "suspect"

[observation.6.11.2307]
task = "6.11"
file = "src/modules/asset/lx_asset_state.c"
symbol = "lx_asset_state_root LX_ASSET_STATE_MAX_ENTRIES sort_entries"
observed = "lx_asset_state_root built its leaf set in two automatic arrays dimensioned LX_ASSET_REGISTRY_CAPACITY + LX_ACCOUNT_REGISTRY_CAPACITY: state_entry entries[...] at 456 bytes each and uint8_t hashes[...][32]. With the account constant at 512 that frame was about half a megabyte; with the reservation ceiling it is 511736880 bytes, and gcc -Wframe-larger-than reports it as the only function in src, cmd or tests whose frame grew with the constant. Every caller overflowed its stack: test-asset-balance, test-asset-withdraw, test-bridge-withdraw, test-gateway-send, test-gateway-receive and test-gateway-kv all died in this frame, confirmed by an AddressSanitizer stack-overflow report naming lx_asset_state_root. Both arrays and a pointer array for the sort order are now heap allocated from assets->count + accounts->count, and the O(n^2) insertion sort over the entries is a qsort over that pointer array whose comparator compares the 64-byte key and breaks ties by original position, which reproduces the stable order the insertion sort produced. The leaf order and therefore the asset state root are unchanged."
assumption = "The whole tree was scanned with -Wframe-larger-than to find every automatic array that the constant sizes, rather than repairing the crashes one test at a time. This is the only one."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.11.2308]
task = "6.11"
file = "tests/modules/test_asset_withdraw_activity.c tests/storage/lxp_test_finality_evidence.c tests/bridge/qualify_credit.py tests/test_genesis_manifest.c"
symbol = "test-asset-withdraw-activity test-finality-evidence test-bridge-credit test-genesis-bootstrap"
observed = "Forty-seven targets covering every test source that names the account registry were run together with make -k on this revision. Four fail, and all four fail identically on an unmodified checkout of main built in a separate worktree, recorded side by side in qual-logs/registry-consumers.log and qual-logs/baseline-consumers.log. They are test-genesis-bootstrap, described in observation 6.11.2306; test-asset-withdraw-activity, whose withdraw dispatch answers LXP_ERR_CONTEXT_MISMATCH at tests/modules/test_asset_withdraw_activity.c:151 in mode 0; test-finality-evidence, which reports multi-activity batch evidence recovery failed; and test-bridge-credit, where build/bin/layerx-genesis-build exits 1 on the --custody-profile request that tests/bridge/qualify_credit.py builds. test-escrow-capture was a fifth failure on the earlier base this change was written against and passes on this one, on the branch and on the baseline alike, so it is no longer carried here. Every other target in the batch passes, including test-replica, test-replay, test-snapshot, test-state-root, test-state-diff, test-state-commitment-transition, the six perps targets, the escrow and stream targets and the gateway targets. The layerx-programs workspace suite that reads the shared account-state vector was run separately and passes."
assumption = "The four are recorded rather than repaired: each reproduces on unmodified main with the same message, and none is about account storage. The three that are not the genesis one have no observation of their own in this ledger yet and are named here so the evidence is not lost."
severity = "suspect"

[observation.1.2.4500]
task = "1.2"
file = "platform/cli/src/install/mcp.rs platform/cli/src/main.rs"
symbol = "platform_install_mcp payment_binding daemon_binding_path"
observed = "`layerx install mcp` still selects and provisions a gateway scoped key (platform/cli/src/install/mcp.rs:43-58 select and LAYERX_GATEWAY_KEY_ID) and still requires `--source-account` and `--asset` outside read-only mode (platform/cli/src/install/mcp.rs:146-165 payment_binding), although the path it installs no longer holds a gateway credential or a local signing seed: the launch line it writes is now `mcp serve --daemon-binding <path> [--read-only]` and every tool call is authorized by the agent daemon named in that document. The installer resolves the binding path as <CLI configuration directory>/mcp/binding.json and records it in the installation report, but nothing in the repository writes that file. The document names the tenant, the store, the audit root, the session and its token file, the capability, the core sequence, the daemon endpoint and the daemon bearer file, so producing it is agent-daemon enrolment work rather than CLI installation work."
assumption = "Retiring the gateway key selection and the two payment flags from `install mcp` has to happen in the same change that makes enrolment write the binding document, because until something writes that file the installed launch line has no input. Removing the flags first would also break the two installed-surface tests in platform/cli/tests/install.rs that pass them, which is a weakening this row will not do."
severity = "blocker"

[observation.1.2.4501]
task = "1.2"
file = "docs/wiki/Cli.md docs/wiki/RunningAnAgent.md"
symbol = "layerx mcp serve"
observed = "The published command reference still documents the retired gateway-bound surface. docs/wiki/Cli.md:78 lists `layerx mcp serve` as requiring `--gateway-credential` with `--environment`, `--key`, `--source-account` and `--asset` optional, and :112 repeats that `--gateway-credential` belongs to `mcp serve`. docs/wiki/RunningAnAgent.md:26 records the served tools as `receipt.get` and `activity.submit` authorized by a hosted gateway `Authorization: LayerX-Key`, :171-179 shows the retired invocation, :264-268 shows the retired launch arguments, and :533-543 tabulates the two-tool surface. The command now takes `--daemon-binding <path>` and `--read-only` only, and serves the twenty-tool daemon catalogue."
assumption = "Documentation is not an owned path for this row, and the wiki cites line ranges in files this row moved, so the rewrite belongs to whoever owns docs/wiki rather than being folded in here."
severity = "note"

[observation.1.2.4502]
task = "1.2"
file = "platform/cli/src/toolset.rs platform/cli/src/a2a.rs platform/cli/src/install/a2a.rs"
symbol = "SERVED Runtime invoke"
observed = "`layerx a2a serve` is now the only CLI surface that still loads a local signing key, holds a gateway credential and posts signed envelopes to the hosted gateway: toolset::SERVED is still the two-tool `receipt.get` and `activity.submit` list, toolset::Runtime still resolves a gateway credential alias and a configured signing key, and platform/cli/src/a2a.rs:100-101 still builds that runtime. The model context protocol path no longer uses any of it. The same daemon-bound binding that this row applied to `mcp serve` would apply to `a2a serve`, but the agent card, the task interface and their installed manifest are a different served path with their own tests, so they were left exactly as written."
assumption = "One served path can be rebound without rebinding the other; leaving the A2A runtime untouched keeps its tests and its installed manifest honest rather than half-migrated."
severity = "note"

[observation.5.1.4503]
task = "5.1"
file = "platform/emulator/build.rs Makefile"
symbol = "programs-build PROGRAMS_CARGO PROGRAMS_RUNTIME_LIB"
observed = "Any cargo command over the platform workspace that reaches layerx-platform-emulator deadlocks against itself whenever CARGO_TARGET_DIR is set. platform/emulator/build.rs:29-31 runs `make build` in the repository root; Makefile:3044-3047 builds the programs runtime with `cd programs && $(PROGRAMS_CARGO) build --locked --workspace --features layerx-programs-sandbox/host-ffi`, and Makefile:3032 leaves PROGRAMS_CARGO as plain `cargo`, so the nested cargo inherits CARGO_TARGET_DIR from the outer cargo and blocks on the build-directory flock the outer cargo is still holding. Observed directly: the outer process held the write lock on <target>/debug/.cargo-lock while two of its own descendants waited on the same lock, and the run made no progress for over forty minutes until it was killed. Exporting PROGRAMS_CARGO as `env CARGO_TARGET_DIR=<repository>/programs/target cargo` before the outer cargo clears it, and keeps the archive at Makefile:3033 PROGRAMS_RUNTIME_LIB, which is a path relative to the repository root rather than to any target directory. Superseded at 8f10a4b46: both remedies this record names are now in place, so the deadlock no longer reproduces at this base. Makefile:3066 defines PROGRAMS_TARGET_DIR as $(CURDIR)/programs/target and Makefile:3082 runs programs-build through PROGRAMS_WORKSPACE_CARGO, which pins CARGO_TARGET_DIR to that directory, while platform/emulator/build.rs:31-32 additionally removes CARGO_TARGET_DIR and CARGO_BUILD_TARGET_DIR from the environment it hands to make. Verified on this revision: `cargo clippy -p layerx-platform-cli --all-targets` over the platform workspace with CARGO_TARGET_DIR set to the shared platform target finished in five minutes with exit 0 and no cargo waiting on an ancestor anywhere in its process tree (qual-logs/cli-clippy.log). The severity is lowered from blocker to note because the condition is resolved rather than tolerated; the original text above is retained unchanged so the record of what was observed stays legible."
assumption = "The build script must not assume it is the only cargo in the process tree. Either the Makefile pins the nested build to programs/target, which is the path PROGRAMS_RUNTIME_LIB already names, or the build script clears CARGO_TARGET_DIR from the environment it hands to make. Setting it per invocation from outside is a workaround, not a fix, because every caller has to know. Both were done at 8f10a4b46, so no caller has to know any more."
severity = "note"

[observation.5.1.4504]
task = "5.1"
file = "agent/crates/layerx-crypto/src/payments.rs agent/Cargo.toml"
symbol = "Disclosure::decode workspace.lints.clippy.pedantic"
observed = "Strict clippy over the agent workspace is red on origin/main b01a0d6df, independently of any change in this branch. agent/Cargo.toml:26 denies clippy::pedantic for every workspace member at priority -1, and clippy::too_many_lines bounds a function at 100 lines. Disclosure::decode at agent/crates/layerx-crypto/src/payments.rs:403 now spans 116 source lines, which clippy counts as 109, so `cargo clippy --locked --manifest-path agent/Cargo.toml -p layerx-mcp --all-targets -- -D warnings` exits 101 with `error: this function has too many lines (109/100)` and `error: could not compile layerx-crypto (lib) due to 1 previous error` before layerx-mcp is ever linted (qual-logs/mcp-clippy.log). The crate is byte-identical to origin/main in this branch: `git diff --quiet origin/main HEAD -- agent/crates/layerx-crypto` exits 0. The function crossed the bound at 9bc3c3cf3, which added 24 lines to payments.rs to route the Asset pause and unpause ordinals through Payment::Pause and Payment::Unpause; that commit's recorded gates ran `cargo test -p layerx-crypto` but no strict clippy over the agent workspace, so the lint regression was not visible to it. Every agent-workspace clippy invocation that compiles layerx-crypto is affected, not only this row's. The platform workspace is unaffected because layerx-crypto is a path dependency there rather than a workspace member, so the deny does not apply to it: `cargo clippy -p layerx-platform-cli --all-targets` exits 0 on the same tree."
assumption = "The bound and the code disagree and both are left exactly as written: the function is not shortened, the lint is not allowed at the call site, and the workspace deny is not relaxed. Repairing it means splitting Disclosure::decode along its existing per-module decode arms, which is layerx-crypto's payment disclosure surface and not this row's, and it should be done by whoever owns that surface rather than by suppressing the diagnostic. Until then the agent workspace has no green strict-clippy gate, and a lane whose row requires one cannot obtain it without touching a crate it does not own."
severity = "blocker"

[observation.5.1.9900]
task = "5.1"
file = "agent/crates/layerx-crypto/src/payments.rs agent/Cargo.toml"
symbol = "Payment::decode decode_registration decode_receive decode_program_transfer decode_program_account"
observed = "The agent workspace denies clippy::pedantic for every member at agent/Cargo.toml:26, which bounds a function at a hundred lines, and Payment::decode at agent/crates/layerx-crypto/src/payments.rs:403 had grown to 116 source lines, counted as 109, so layerx-crypto failed to compile under clippy and every agent-workspace clippy invocation stopped before reaching the crate it was aimed at. This is the condition recorded as observation 5.1.4504 on an unmerged branch. The bound is restored by decomposition rather than suppression: the four long match arms now live in named decoders beside decode_grant, decode_registration at payments.rs:487, decode_receive at 500, decode_program_transfer at 526 and decode_program_account at 544, and decode itself is 55 source lines. No allow attribute was added, the workspace deny is untouched and the hundred-line bound is unchanged. The wire behaviour is identical: each helper keeps its field evaluation order, decode_receive keeps the short-circuiting frame check that reads the second u16 only when the first equals 0x5201, decode_program_transfer keeps the zero and 256 leg bounds ahead of any leg read, decode_program_account keeps the LXPA1 marker check, and decode keeps the version prefix guard, the unsupported-activity refusal and the re-encode comparison against the original payload. The existing fixture suite is the oracle and still passes unchanged, including the exhaustive byte-flip, truncation and trailing-byte cases over 1-2.hex and 1-3.hex; five tests were added at agent/crates/layerx-crypto/tests/payments.rs:363-461 that pin the boundaries the helpers now own, each payload derived from an existing fixture or the encoder rather than from a new fixture. Evidence: qual-logs/crypto-clippy.log exit 0 and qual-logs/crypto-tests.log exit 0 with 49 tests passing, both in a lane-private target directory."
assumption = "Splitting the decoder along its existing per-activity arms is a behaviour-preserving move, and the round-trip comparison in decode plus the fixture oracle prove it; the lint bound and the code now agree without either being relaxed."
severity = "note"

[observation.5.1.9901]
task = "5.1"
file = "tests/support/lxgb_metadata.rs"
symbol = "append custody_reference"
observed = "With layerx-crypto compiling cleanly under clippy the agent-workspace gate advances past it and stops on the shared genesis metadata fixture, which is a test-support module thirteen layerx-agentd integration targets include through agent/crates/layerx-agentd/tests/support. Six pedantic denials fire in it: clippy::format_collect at tests/support/lxgb_metadata.rs:14, clippy::cast_possible_truncation at 18, 36 and 48 for usize-to-u16 casts of a did length and two record lengths, and clippy::expect_used at 22 and 78. Every one of budget_create, budget_divergence, budget_reconcile, budget_unknown, ceiling, limits-exactly-once, migration, operator, outbox, protocol2_restart, protocol_evidence, recovery and unknown fails to compile with the same six errors, so `cargo clippy --locked --manifest-path agent/Cargo.toml --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings` exits 101. The file is byte-identical to the merge base in this branch, `git diff --quiet origin/main HEAD -- tests/support/lxgb_metadata.rs` exits 0, so the denials are properties of that unchanged source and are not introduced here. Evidence: qual-logs/agent-clippy.log for the workspace gate and qual-logs/agent-clippy-keep-going.log for the same command with --keep-going, run first in an empty lane-private target so that it enumerates every cause instead of stopping at the first failing target."
assumption = "The shared genesis metadata fixture is owned by the work that emits the accepted asset record shape, not by the payment disclosure decoder, so the denials are reported with their exact lints and locations rather than repaired here; each has a behaviour-preserving repair available, a fold with write! for the hex string, a checked conversion for the three length casts and a let-else refusal for the two expect calls."
severity = "blocker"

[observation.5.1.9902]
task = "5.1"
file = "agent/crates/layerx-client/tests/supply_receipt.rs"
symbol = "native_supply_receipt_roundtrip_and_authenticated_tampering"
observed = "The second remaining cause of the red agent-workspace clippy gate is clippy::expect_used at agent/crates/layerx-client/tests/supply_receipt.rs:32 and :33, which the workspace denies at agent/Cargo.toml alongside unwrap_used. The rest of the same file already uses the let-else refusal form that satisfies the deny, at lines 12 to 26, so only the two calls in the test body are affected and layerx-client fails to compile the supply_receipt target. The file is byte-identical to the merge base in this branch, `git diff --quiet origin/main HEAD -- agent/crates/layerx-client/tests/supply_receipt.rs` exits 0. These two causes are the only ones left: the keep-going run over an empty lane-private target checks every target of every workspace member, layerx-types, layerx-wire, layerx-crypto, layerx-proof, layerx-client, layerx-agent-api, layerx-agentd, layerx-mcp and layerx-sdk, and reports no clippy error outside these two files. Evidence: qual-logs/agent-clippy-keep-going.log."
assumption = "A crate outside the payment disclosure surface is not repaired from this change; the two calls need the same let-else form the file already uses elsewhere, which weakens nothing."
severity = "blocker"

[observation.4.1.10100]
task = "4.1"
file = "interop/Cargo.lock agent/crates/layerx-sdk/Cargo.toml interop/Cargo.toml interop/deploy/mirror/Dockerfile"
symbol = "cargo metadata --locked tungstenite 0.30.0"
observed = "interop/Cargo.lock was regenerated by a plain cargo resolution against the unchanged manifests; cargo reported Locking 14 packages to latest compatible versions. Seven names are new to the lock: data-encoding 2.11.1, hybrid-array 0.4.15, rand 0.10.2, sha1 0.11.0, thiserror 2.0.20, thiserror-impl 2.0.20 and tungstenite 0.30.0. Seven already-locked packages gained a second version beside the one they carried: block-buffer 0.10.4 gained 0.12.1, chacha20 0.9.1 gained 0.10.2, const-oid 0.9.6 gained 0.10.2, cpufeatures 0.2.17 gained 0.3.1, crypto-common 0.1.6 gained 0.2.2, digest 0.10.7 gained 0.11.3 and rand_core 0.6.4 gained 0.10.1. Nothing was removed and no locked version was replaced; the whole delta is the transitive closure of the tungstenite =0.30.0 requirement at agent/crates/layerx-sdk/Cargo.toml:20 that reaches the interop graph through the layerx-intents path dependency at interop/Cargo.toml:31. cargo metadata --manifest-path interop/Cargo.toml --locked now exits 0 where it exited 101 with the lock file needs to be updated, so the --locked commands in both RUN lines of interop/deploy/mirror/Dockerfile, .github/workflows/mirror-live.yml:29, .github/workflows/mirror-verification-live.yml:26 and interop/README.md:64-65 resolve on this revision, and cargo build --locked --manifest-path interop/Cargo.toml -p layerx-mirror compiles the package to completion (exit 0, lane-private target). This supersedes the resolution half of blocker 4.1.9801; the ban half is recorded as 4.1.10101."
assumption = "The lock was the stale side rather than the manifest: agent/Cargo.lock, human/Cargo.lock and platform/Cargo.lock all carry the same SDK requirement and resolve under --locked unchanged, and a lock that cannot be resolved is not a statement about which dependencies are wanted."
severity = "note"

[observation.4.1.10101]
task = "4.1"
file = "interop/deny.toml interop/Cargo.lock interop/tools/dependency-policy.sh"
symbol = "bans multiple-versions deny advisories RUSTSEC-2024-0436"
observed = "The dependency gate the repository runs over this lock is make interop-lint, which is sh interop/tools/dependency-policy.sh followed by cargo deny --manifest-path interop/Cargo.toml check advisories bans sources. The policy script exits 0 on the regenerated lock: no forbidden boundary or global-initialisation crate entered the graph, every source package carries an allowlisted SPDX licence, and interop/crates holds no unsafe. cargo deny exits 3 and prints advisories FAILED, bans FAILED, sources ok. interop/deny.toml sets multiple-versions = deny with one skip, syn =2.0.119, so every duplicate is refused by name: error[duplicate]: found 2 duplicate entries for crate block-buffer, chacha20, const-oid, cpufeatures, crypto-common, der, digest, getrandom, rand_core and windows-sys, plus error[unmaintained]: paste - no longer maintained for paste 1.0.15 (RUSTSEC-2024-0436, reached through wasmi_core 0.13.0 and wasmi 0.31.2 under layerx-programs-runtime; the advisory records that no safe upgrade is available). The der, getrandom, windows-sys and syn duplicates and the paste advisory are already present in main's lock and are untouched by this change. The seven remaining duplicates are the ones the tungstenite closure introduces, and they are not introduced by committing the lock: cargo deny run on a checkout carrying main's stale lock regenerates that lock itself into content byte-identical with the one committed here and prints exactly the same ten duplicate errors and the same advisory, also exit 3 (qual-logs/interop-cargo-deny-baseline-main-lock.log). deny.toml was not edited, no skip and no advisory ignore was added, and the gate is left refusing."
assumption = "The ban list is the authority and the lock is the reported fact, so clearing this needs the workspace crypto stack moved onto the crypto-common 0.2 and digest 0.11 major line, or a tungstenite release built on the majors already locked, which is a change to agent and interop manifests outside this issue."
severity = "blocker"

[observation.4.1.10102]
task = "4.1"
file = "platform/hosted/tests/beta-images.sh platform/release/registries.kvx platform/hosted/tests/publish-images.sh"
symbol = "IMAGE_NAMES image_source image_registry.ghcr images"
observed = "layerx-mirror is now in both halves of the published image set, as observation 4.1.9802 spells out. platform/hosted/tests/beta-images.sh carries layerx-mirror in IMAGE_NAMES and the image_source case layerx-mirror) printf 'ghcr.io/sidiora-labs/layerx-mirror:0.1.0 interop/deploy/mirror/Dockerfile', in the same form as the seventeen entries beside it; the Dockerfile path is resolved against the whole tracked tree that beta-cluster.sh build_context packs, which is the context every image is built from. platform/release/registries.kvx:90 carries layerx-mirror in the [image_registry.ghcr] images list, inserted between layerx-internal and layerx-node because layerx_platform_release::sorted_identities at platform/release/src/main.rs:434-445 refuses a list that is not strictly ascending. The two lists name the same eighteen images. make platform-release-check exits 0 and tools/ci/beta-contract-check.sh exits 0; the manifest image agreement at tools/ci/beta-contract-check.sh:990-1025 stays inert because platform/release/artifact-manifest.json is absent and the contract states artifact_manifest_status not_emitted, so the eighteenth name enters both declarations before any manifest exists to contradict. publish-images.sh iterates the same IMAGE_NAMES for its inventory, digest record and pull checks, so the publisher now requires eighteen built images; the remaining gate on actually publishing this one is the ban refusal recorded in 4.1.10101, not the image tables."
assumption = "An image is published only when the image table and the release registry declaration agree, so both files change together and neither is a partial claim."
severity = "note"

[observation.4.1.10103]
task = "4.1"
file = "interop/deploy/mirror/kubernetes.yaml platform/release/registries.kvx platform/hosted/identity/deployment.yaml"
symbol = "publisher container image reference"
observed = "interop/deploy/mirror/kubernetes.yaml:22 pinned ghcr.io/layerx/layerx-mirror-publisher@sha256:REPLACE_WITH_PINNED_DIGEST, which named neither the canonical organisation, nor the image the Dockerfile publishes under, nor a real digest. It now reads ghcr.io/sidiora-labs/layerx-mirror:0.1.0, the same reference form platform/hosted/identity/deployment.yaml:19 and the other hosted deployment manifests use and exactly the canonical reference beta-images.sh image_source records for this image. No digest was invented: a registry manifest digest exists only after the image is pushed, and platform/release/registries.kvx declares verification = digest-pinned-registry-manifest for this registry, so the manifest has to move from the tag to <repository>@<digest> once the first publication of layerx-mirror records one. That publication is blocked by 4.1.10101."
assumption = "A tag reference that names the real repository is an honest statement of an unpublished image, while a placeholder digest is not, and the digest pin is a follow-up that only the first publish can supply."
severity = "note"

[observation.3.3.8200]
task = "3.3"
file = "include/layerx/lxp_result.h src/modules/service/lx_service_state.c tests/modules/test_service_acceptance.c tests/modules/test_service_dispute.c"
symbol = "LXP_ERR_DUPLICATE_ENTRY lx_service_hashes_check lxp_result_name LXP_RESULT_CODE_LIST"
observed = "lx_service_hashes_check (src/modules/service/lx_service_state.c:99-113) refused three distinct malformed inputs with one number: a null set, an empty or oversized count, a zero hash, and a repeated hash all returned LXP_ERR_NON_CANONICAL, so a caller could not tell a duplicate entry from a malformed one. The canonical set now has a dedicated number: LXP_ERR_DUPLICATE_ENTRY = -8 is declared in include/layerx/lxp_result.h:38, immediately after LXP_ERR_UNKNOWN_FIELD = -7 and inside the codec band that LXP_ERR_NON_CANONICAL and LXP_ERR_UNSORTED_SEQUENCE already occupy, and the repeated-hash branch at src/modules/service/lx_service_state.c:110 is the only refusal changed. Because the header declares codes through LXP_RESULT_CODE_LIST, the string mapping in src/protocol/lxp_result.c:3-12 and the declarations in include/layerx/lxp_result.h:160-162 pick the new code up with no separate table edit, and tests/protocol/lxp_test_result.c:12-59 then covers it automatically: it asserts lxp_result_name returns the exact identifier, that lxp_result_domain places -8 in LXP_RESULT_DOMAIN_CODEC, and that no two declared codes share a number. Every caller of lx_service_hashes_check propagates the status unchanged, so all seven paths that validate a hash set report the new code: the reject and dispute-open payload decoders (src/modules/service/lx_service_payload.c:216 and 241), the dispute-open executor's re-check of the request evidence (src/modules/service/lx_service_dispute.c:45), the agreement record encoder and decoder (lx_service_state.c:302 and 365) and the dispute record encoder and decoder (lx_service_state.c:933 and 992). Tests: duplicate contested hashes through service.reject dispatch and duplicate evidence hashes through service.dispute_open dispatch now assert LXP_ERR_DUPLICATE_ENTRY (tests/modules/test_service_acceptance.c:229, tests/modules/test_service_dispute.c:400); each is paired with a zero-hash case in the same set that still asserts LXP_ERR_NON_CANONICAL, so the two failures are proven distinct at the same boundary; lx_service_reject_execute with two identical contested hashes that were both genuinely delivered is refused at the record encoder and leaves the agreement DELIVERED with no contested hash stored (test_service_acceptance.c:279-286); and both record codecs are driven directly, refusing a duplicate on encode and on decode while a zero in the same position still refuses with LXP_ERR_NON_CANONICAL (test_service_acceptance.c:297-322, test_service_dispute.c:442-464). Build and test were not run in this lane: neither make CC=gcc test nor the focused test-result and test-service-* targets completed here, so no gate result is claimed for the C changes and the owner's build on main is the first to compile and run them."
assumption = "Result numbers are consensus data, so the code was appended rather than inserted into an occupied position and no existing value moved; -8 was taken inside the codec band instead of after the last declared number because lxp_result_domain derives the domain from the numeric range alone and a duplicate entry in a canonical set is a codec failure, not a fatal one. Only the duplicate branch changed: the null, empty, oversized and zero-hash refusals keep LXP_ERR_NON_CANONICAL, and no existing assertion that pins that code for a genuinely non-canonical input was touched. The declared paths of this change name include/layerx/lxp_errors.h and src/protocol/lxp_errors*.c; no such files exist, and the enumeration and its string mapping live in include/layerx/lxp_result.h and src/protocol/lxp_result.c, which are the files used."
severity = "note"

[observation.3.3.8201]
task = "3.3"
file = "src/modules/perps/lx_perps_market.c src/ledger/lxp_journal.c src/sequencer/lxp_eligibility.c src/state/lxp_state_root.c src/guarantor/lxp_bond_state.c"
symbol = "LXP_ERR_DUPLICATE_ENTRY LXP_ERR_NON_CANONICAL accounts_canonical market_validate lxp_eligibility_init lxp_state_root_compute"
observed = "src/modules/service is the only module that reports a repeated entry in a canonical set as LXP_ERR_NON_CANONICAL, so no other module needed the new code. The one other pairwise duplicate scan under src/modules, accounts_canonical in src/modules/perps/lx_perps_market.c:33-49, folds a zero or repeated account identity into a single validity flag that market_validate (lx_perps_market.c:51-77) reports as LXP_ERR_PARAMETER_BOUNDS together with every other market parameter refusal, and the strictly-ascending checks in src/modules/perps/lx_perps_command.c:396-398 and 428-430 and src/modules/governance/lxp_gov_param.c:51-53 and 97-99 report an equal neighbour as LXP_ERR_UNSORTED_SEQUENCE; none of them names a duplicate as non-canonical. Six refusals in four files outside src/modules do report a duplicate as LXP_ERR_NON_CANONICAL and were left as they are: a repeated authorized_from in the journal source-authority set (src/ledger/lxp_journal.c:91-95), a repeated replica identity in the eligibility set (src/sequencer/lxp_eligibility.c:17-19), a repeated account identity while enumerating state-root leaves (src/state/lxp_state_root.c:294-297), and three in the guarantor bond set - a repeated guarantor identity (src/guarantor/lxp_bond_state.c:88-91), the same signer public key held by two records (lxp_bond_state.c:96-103) and a public key already authorised at src/guarantor/lxp_bond_state.c:214-218. Each would read more precisely as LXP_ERR_DUPLICATE_ENTRY now that the number exists."
assumption = "Recorded rather than changed: the four files that report a duplicate as LXP_ERR_NON_CANONICAL lie outside the declared paths of this change, as does the perps market scan, so converting those refusals belongs to the owners of the ledger, sequencer, state-root and guarantor areas. Nothing depends on the conversion - the new code is additive and the existing number stays valid for those sites."
severity = "note"

[observation.3.3.8202]
task = "3.3"
file = "agent/crates/layerx-types/src/result.rs agent/crates/layerx-types/build.rs"
symbol = "protocol_result_codes DuplicateEntry KnownResult"
observed = "agent/crates/layerx-types/build.rs:120-146 compares the C LXP_RESULT_CODE_LIST table with the protocol_result_codes! mirror position by position and panics the build on the first divergence, so declaring LXP_ERR_DUPLICATE_ENTRY = -8 in the header without the mirror entry stops layerx-types and every crate above it from compiling: the gate reports that the header declares DuplicateEntry = -8 where src/result.rs declares WrongNetwork = -100. The mirror therefore carries DuplicateEntry = -8, Terminal at agent/crates/layerx-types/src/result.rs:88, directly after UnknownField = -7 and in the same order as the header. Terminal is the classification the code already received before it was named - ResultCode::retriability (result.rs:252-257) treats every number the mirror does not know as Terminal, and ResultCode::domain (result.rs:232-248) already placed -8 in ResultDomain::Codec from its numeric range - so the entry adds the typed variant and its name without changing how any agent classifies the refusal. Gate: CARGO_TARGET_DIR pointed at a lane-private directory, cargo test -p layerx-types exit 0."
assumption = "The crate lies outside the declared paths of this change and the single line was taken because the parity gate makes the header change unbuildable without it; the C table stays normative and the mirror follows it, which is the rule the parity gate already records. Nothing else in the crate is touched."
severity = "note"
[observation.6.1.9000]
task = "6.1"
file = "platform/hosted/node/bootstrap.sh agent/crates/layerx-client/tests/availability_daemon.rs docs/wiki/HostedNode.md"
symbol = "RETAINED_REQUEST assert_genesis_request built_interface_version"
observed = "Both refusals recorded against the availability daemon harness at 6.1.8604 and 6.1.8601 are addressed at this revision. platform/hosted/node/bootstrap.sh:504-505 now moves the LXGB v2 request from the scratch directory to genesis/genesis-request.lxgb once layerx-genesis-build has produced the manifest, snapshot, registration request and deployment descriptor from it and before the scratch directory that also held the genesis signer key is removed at the end of the bootstrap, so a completed data directory retains the exact request its genesis was built from at the mode the bootstrap already gave it, and the header list of outputs and docs/wiki/HostedNode.md name it. assert_genesis_request at agent/crates/layerx-client/tests/availability_daemon.rs:305-310 reads that retained copy in place of the removed data/work/genesis-request.lxgb and additionally requires the scratch directory to be absent; every length, envelope version, parameter count, guarantor order, key prefix, bond and guarantor key assertion it carried is unchanged. The seven Version::V1_4 pins in that harness at lines 21, 47, 95, 117, 177, 194 and 202 (the refusal envelope, both FetchContext values, the finality evidence registration, the HandshakeConfig built_interface_version and both batch header lookups) now read Version::V1_5, the revision agent/crates/layerx-client/src/lni/schema.rs:440 and cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_lni.c:48 declare. On the wire the change is the request envelope minor alone: decode_envelope at cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_lni.c:1179-1181 accepts any minor up to 5, perform at agent/crates/layerx-client/src/lni/handshake.rs:132-135 still sends the node info request at minor 0, every daemon response carries the daemon revision, and the only minor 5 selector branch, wait_publication in send_receipt at cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_lni.c:2148, is on a message this harness never sends; the 10 byte batch header selector and the 9, 17 and 33 byte availability selectors it does send are read identically at minor 4 and 5. platform/hosted/node/tests/bootstrap-test.py:38-45 opens the request while the bootstrap is still running and keeps the descriptor, so the move does not affect what it reads. Build and test were not run in this lane: make CC=gcc test-daemon-availability and its three consecutive runs, make beta-ledger-check and the platform node tests that run the bootstrap were not executed here and no gate result is claimed."
assumption = "The bootstrap decides what a node keeps and the harness reads what the bootstrap leaves, so the request is retained beside the artefacts derived from it rather than the assertion being pointed at a transient path or the scratch directory being kept; the request carries public guarantor keys, the asset identifier, the metering and fee parameters and the metadata suffix and no secret, so retaining it under the 0700 data directory widens nothing, and the supervisor reset discards it with the rest of the data directory through --force."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.1.9001]
task = "6.1"
file = "tests/daemon/lxp_test_program_admission.c agent/schema/lni/v1.kvx cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_lni.c"
symbol = "availability_batches wait_publication send_receipt"
observed = "The 2 second receipt poll recorded at 6.1.8605 is unchanged at this revision: availability_batches at tests/daemon/lxp_test_program_admission.c:677-693 still sends the receipt lookup up to 200 times with a 10 millisecond pause where checked_receipt and governance_registration pause 50 milliseconds. The interface the client now declares settles what the contract requires instead of either budget: agent/schema/lni/v1.kvx:50 states that minor 5 permits a trailing wait_publication byte on the receipt lookup selector under which the node waits on queue and publication events until found, drained, failed or the request deadline, and never a fixed polling interval, and send_receipt at cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_lni.c:2148-2152 implements that flag for a 34 byte activity selector. The native client already declares LNI_MINOR 5 at tests/daemon/lxp_test_program_admission.c:51, so the poll is a client construction the revision it speaks tells it not to use, not a bound the contract sets at 2 or at 10 seconds. The file is outside the paths this row owns and was not edited; build and test were not run in this lane."
assumption = "Aligning the pause to 50 milliseconds would replace one fixed polling interval with another; the change the schema calls for is a 34 byte selector with wait_publication set and the request deadline as the bound, which belongs to the owner of the native admission client."
severity = "suspect"

[observation.6.2.6000]
task = "6.2"
file = "platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/lifecycle.rs platform/hosted/core/src/public_reads.rs platform/hosted/gateway/src/rpc.rs"
symbol = "assert_unavailable_reads local_gateway_rpc local_gateway_committed_payment_reads assert_committed_fee_reads estimate_fee assets"
observed = "assert_unavailable_reads requires JSON-RPC -32001 from lx_listAssets [], lx_getAsset, lx_estimateFee [\"abcd\"] and lx_getBalances. It was written at 9dc9ddecf39d391551f6f2b97172a71a47210b9a, whose message records that native asset, fee and DID read integration was still required. At that revision the route function in platform/hosted/core/src/public_reads.rs had no /v1/assets and no /v1/fees/estimate arm, so both selectors fell through to protocol_route and answered 404, and read_response in platform/hosted/gateway/src/rpc.rs maps every non-200 upstream that is not 400, 415, 401, 403 or 429 to -32001. 57dbd454c0287162d1dd6198200d25b8588f74b1 then added both core routes, and e5ad40e8d1af58acde9c1578b09da8cf76d36725 added local_gateway_committed_payment_reads, which requires lx_listAssets [] to resolve with exactly one asset and requires assert_committed_fee_reads to receive -32602 from lx_estimateFee [\"abcd\"], the identical method and parameters that assert_unavailable_reads requires to be -32001. Both tests assemble the same gateway, identity, authority, Redis and core boundary through the same start_local_gateway and gateway_upstream_environment helpers and issue the reads unauthenticated over POST /rpc, so the gateway configuration is identical. estimate_fee decodes the canonical activity before it connects to the node, so lx_estimateFee [\"abcd\"] answers 400 invalid_activity and -32602 independently of cluster state. Nothing the hosted core observes separates the two clusters at read time: send_node_info in cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_lni.c advertises asset_read and fee_estimate for every role, lxp_daemon_finality_authority_init is mandatory at startup so both clusters advertise finality_evidence_register, cmd/layerx-genesis/lxp_genesis_builder.c writes a non-zero current_state_root in both, and finalise_first_batch in platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/funding.rs, the only path that makes latest_finalised_checkpoint non-zero, is not called by local_gateway_committed_payment_reads. The single core-visible difference is the Bridge custody-issued value that lx_asset_committed_records folds into total_units, which the asset record carries in both clusters either way."
assumption = "The asset and fee halves of assert_unavailable_reads pin the pre-integration behaviour of two selectors that the core has since implemented. Restoring -32001 for lx_listAssets and lx_estimateFee would require removing the core asset and fee routes or gating them on a fabricated cluster signal, and either would contradict the committed-read assertions in the same file, so both checks are left exactly as written and neither test is edited. The lx_getBalances part of the same assertion is the one half a real contract decides, because an empty DID account listing carries no evidence of absence; that reconciliation is the core boundary DID account work recorded at observation 6.11.161 and is not duplicated here."
severity = "blocker"

[observation.6.2.6001]
task = "6.2"
file = "platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/lifecycle.rs tests/support/lxgb_metadata.rs tests/support/lxgb_metadata.py platform/hosted/core/tests/boundary.rs"
symbol = "local_gateway_committed_payment_reads append genesis_request funded_genesis metadata"
observed = "local_gateway_committed_payment_reads asserts that the listed asset symbol equals \"TEST\". Both LXGB metadata producers encode a three-byte symbol: tests/support/lxgb_metadata.rs and tests/support/lxgb_metadata.py both emit the length byte 3 followed by TST. genesis_request in platform/hosted/core/tests/boundary.rs appends that record for every cluster, funded_genesis in platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/funding.rs reuses genesis_request and only patches the fee schedule bytes, and the local harness build script rewrites the metadata include to that same single file, so no cluster carries a different record. The metadata decoder in agent/crates/layerx-client/src/payments.rs copies exactly symbol_length bytes and asset_json in platform/hosted/core/src/public_reads.rs reports them verbatim, so the read answers TST. The explicit metadata producers were introduced at 72c98627117d0101b99f135765b4602cb11b739c, before the assertion was written, and the genesis request carried no asset metadata record at all on the parent revision."
assumption = "The expected symbol predates the explicit metadata producers, so the committed payment read gate cannot pass on this revision for a reason unrelated to the unavailable-reads contract. The assertion and both producers are left unchanged, because changing either would alter a check rather than reconcile the metadata contract that owns the symbol."
severity = "blocker"

[observation.6.2.6002]
task = "6.2"
file = "platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/lifecycle.rs platform/hosted/core/src/public_reads.rs agent/crates/layerx-client/src/lni/handshake.rs platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/funding.rs platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/checkpoint_settlement.py"
symbol = "assert_unavailable_reads local_gateway_rpc local_gateway_committed_payment_reads assets estimate_fee NodeInfo latest_sealed_batch latest_finalised_checkpoint"
observed = "A runtime probe of the cluster local_gateway_rpc builds (start_cluster(true), start_boundary, establish_receipt_head) polled /v1/node-info every three seconds for one hundred and thirty-eight seconds. latest_sealed_batch read 0 before establish_receipt_head and at 6.2 s, then 1 from 9.8 s to the end of the run; chain_head_sequence read 2 from 6.2 s onward; latest_finalised_checkpoint stayed all zero for the whole run. Driving the same cluster through the exact prefix of local_gateway_rpc (identity, receipt authority, Redis, gateway, scoped key, unauthenticated POST /rpc) answered lx_listAssets [] with result.verification authenticated_committed_snapshot and one asset whose asset_id equals cluster.asset, lx_getAsset with -32001 asset_evidence_unavailable, lx_estimateFee [\"abcd\"] with -32602 invalid_activity, lx_getBalances [\"did:layerx:alice\"] with -32001 did_account_listing_unavailable when the core boundary carries the DID account listing refusal, and lx_subscribe with -32004, so assert_unavailable_reads fails first on lx_listAssets at lifecycle.rs:936 with a null error code. The fields a core read can gate on are the NodeInfo members at agent/crates/layerx-client/src/lni/handshake.rs:45-55: interface_version, protocol_version, network_id, role, chain_head_sequence, latest_sealed_batch, latest_finalised_checkpoint, authorised_sequencer_key and advertised_capabilities. latest_finalised_checkpoint is all zero in both clusters because checkpoint_settlement.py blocks on checkpoint-output/available-header.bin (checkpoint_settlement.py:165-170) and only finalise_first_batch writes that file (funding.rs:128), which local_gateway_committed_payment_reads never calls. latest_sealed_batch is at least 1 in both, required of the funded cluster at funding.rs:111 and measured at 1 within ten seconds of establish_receipt_head in the plain cluster. The remaining members come from the same daemon binary and the same genesis builder in both clusters. A guard on latest_sealed_batch != 0 was written into assets and estimate_fee, built into layerx-core-boundary and run end to end: it restores -32001 only inside the window before the first batch seals, local_gateway_rpc still fails at lifecycle.rs:936, and the guard was removed rather than left in place."
assumption = "No precondition drawn from the LNI handshake can answer -32001 for lx_estimateFee [\"abcd\"] in the cluster local_gateway_rpc builds and -32602 for the identical call at lifecycle.rs:2209, because every handshake member the funded cluster satisfies the plain cluster also satisfies at the point of the assertion. This measurement is recorded in support of observation 6.2.6000 and changes nothing: both assertions are left exactly as written, no core route is gated on a fabricated cluster signal, and the read-path change on this branch is limited to giving each JSON-RPC refusal code the message it names."
severity = "blocker"

[observation.6.2.6003]
task = "6.2"
file = "platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/funding.rs platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/lifecycle.rs"
symbol = "funding start credit_node local_gateway_committed_payment_reads"
observed = "funding::start credits the treasury over the real LNI before any hosted service starts. Running it in the local harness on this revision stops at platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/funding.rs:535, which requires LNI frame tag 6 and observed tag 25, the refusal envelope, so the daemon refused the custody credit activity that carries the attested deposit. Every test that reaches its assertions through funding::start, including local_gateway_committed_payment_reads, stops there before a single read is issued."
assumption = "Recorded rather than repaired because the refusal happens inside the funded harness and the daemon credit path, both outside the unavailable-reads contract this change owns. The consequence for that contract is that the committed payment read assertions cannot be exercised at all on this revision, so the only executable half of the conflict described in observation 6.2.6000 is local_gateway_rpc."
severity = "blocker"

[observation.6.2.5900]
task = "6.2"
file = "platform/hosted/core/tests/boundary.rs platform/hosted/node/bootstrap.sh platform/hosted/node/supervisor.sh contracts/config/checkpoint-settlement.json"
symbol = "supervised_files start_supervised_cluster cluster_artifacts SOURCE_ROOT SETTLEMENT_DOCUMENT run_bootstrap"
observed = "The supervisor reset failure recorded at observation 6.11.160 is in the harness staging rather than in the script. supervised_files copies bootstrap.sh and supervisor.sh out of platform/hosted/node into the disposable root under the temporary directory, so the SOURCE_ROOT that bootstrap.sh derives from its own location at lines 279-286 names that root and never the repository, contracts/config/checkpoint-settlement.json is not found, and the /opt/layerx/checkpoint-settlement.json fallback exists only inside the image built by platform/hosted/node/Dockerfile. Naming the repository path in the copied script would not help either: the harness runs every daemon under DAEMON_UID 65534 at boundary.rs:52 and the checkout sits under a 0700 home directory, so that path is unreadable to the process that would open it. cluster_artifacts already copies migrations/0007_history_index.sql into the same disposable root for the same reason, and platform/hosted/node/tests/bootstrap-test.py and platform/hosted/tests/beta-cluster.sh already hand the script an explicit --settlement-document. The harness now copies the shipped settlement document into the disposable root at 0644 and passes that path through the existing --settlement-document argument, so the real script reads the real document; supervisor.sh needs no change because its reset path calls run_bootstrap --force with the whole bootstrap argument list at line 375 and replays the argument with the rest. The shipped certificate threshold is 2, so the request the script assembles carries two guarantors and depends on the threshold-derived length bound landed as a2c5c4b02; against the single-guarantor constant it preceded, the same run failed with 'genesis request has an unexpected length'."
assumption = "The settlement document is an input the caller owns, so supplying it is staging rather than a change to the bootstrap contract. No line of bootstrap.sh or supervisor.sh was touched and no assertion in the test body was changed."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.2.5901]
task = "6.2"
file = "platform/hosted/core/src/public_reads.rs platform/hosted/core/tests/boundary.rs cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_lni.c agent/crates/layerx-client/src/read.rs platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/lifecycle.rs"
symbol = "did_accounts send_did_accounts lx_account_list_did did_account_listing_unavailable public_read_selectors_use_real_node_and_refuse_missing_evidence"
observed = "The DID account listing contract recorded at observation 6.2.940, observation 6.11.161 and observation 6.2.4101 is resolved in favour of the refusal. send_did_accounts at cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_lni.c:3084 answers lx_account_list_did with count zero both for a DID the node has never seen and for a DID that owns nothing, and carries no absence proof in either case, so the 200 the route served with an empty account list and verification authenticated_node_snapshot asserted evidence the node never produced. did_accounts now refuses an empty listing with the typed 503 did_account_listing_unavailable the route already returns for an undecodable listing, which is the shape boundary.rs requires at the /v1/dids/did:layerx:alice/accounts assertion and the shape the gateway maps to -32001, and it reads the listing at STATE_PROVEN instead of UNVERIFIED so every entry it does serve carries proof material, which layerx-client read.rs:835-847 already refuses to return without, verifying each entry against the requested level. The positive path keeps its coverage in platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/lifecycle.rs, where a funded treasury DID lists accounts with per-entry proof; the core harness cannot assert a served listing because a fresh genesis carries no funded treasury account. The verification label on the served body is unchanged because platform/hosted/gateway/openrpc.json and docs/wiki/PublicRpc.md name authenticated_node_snapshot for this route."
assumption = "A listing with no absence proof cannot be distinguished from an unavailable listing, so the refusal is the honest answer and the refusal shape was already defined by the route. The daemon function, the gateway mapping and the boundary assertion were left exactly as written."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.2.5902]
task = "6.2"
file = "platform/hosted/core/tests/boundary.rs tests/support/lxgb_metadata.rs src/modules/asset/lx_asset_registry.c cmd/layerx-genesis/lxp_genesis_build_cli.c"
symbol = "build_genesis genesis_request lxgb_metadata::append issuer_valid lx_asset_record_decode"
observed = "cargo test -p layerx-platform-core --test boundary exits 101 on this revision with none of the fourteen tests passing, for the reason recorded at observation 6.1.5801. Every test builds genesis through tests/support/lxgb_metadata.rs, whose version 3 asset record carries custody kind 1, an empty custody reference and issuer kind 2; issuer_valid at src/modules/asset/lx_asset_registry.c:561-572 requires the custody kind to equal the issuer kind and a non-empty reference for issuer kind 2, so lx_asset_record_decode refuses it and parse_request fails. layerx-genesis-build then exits 1 with nothing on standard error, thirteen tests panic at boundary.rs:144 with an empty failure message and supervisor_reset_rebuilds_genesis_and_replays_once panics at boundary.rs:803 with the script reporting only that layerx-genesis-build refused the genesis request, which is one step past the settlement document probe and the request length assertion that used to stop it. Re-running the same gate with the fixture emitting the only shape the builder accepts, custody kind 2 with a non-empty custody reference and nothing else changed, exits 0 with fourteen of fourteen tests passing, including supervisor_reset_rebuilds_genesis_and_replays_once and public_read_selectors_use_real_node_and_refuse_missing_evidence. Evidence: qual-logs/boundary-main-fixture.log and qual-logs/boundary-probe-fixture.log."
assumption = "The fixture is a shared root test helper owned by the lane that also regenerates every genesis state root derived from it, so the probe shape was reverted and no part of it is proposed here."
severity = "blocker"

[observation.6.2.5903]
task = "6.2"
file = "cmd/layerx-genesis/lxp_genesis_build_cli.c platform/hosted/core/tests/boundary.rs"
symbol = "lxp_genesis_builder_cli_main build_artifacts command"
observed = "layerx-genesis-build reports no diagnostic for any refused request. lxp_genesis_builder_cli_main returns 2 for a wrong argument count and otherwise the result of build_artifacts mapped to 0 or 1, and build_artifacts threads a single status through roughly fifteen steps - reading the request and signer key, parse_request, arena initialisation, the fresh build, the registration and descriptor encodings, five path joins, the output directory and three exclusive writes - without printing which one refused or which lxp_result it returned. Every caller therefore sees exit 1 with an empty stderr: the boundary harness panics at boundary.rs:144 with the program, its arguments and an empty failure message, and bootstrap.sh reports only 'layerx-genesis-build refused the genesis request'. Identifying a refused metadata record required decoding the request bytes by hand against the module's decoder. Printing the failing step and the numeric result on standard error would make every one of those callers self-diagnosing."
assumption = "The CLI is outside this row's files and the missing output changes no behaviour, so it is recorded for the owner of the genesis tooling."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.2.5904]
task = "6.2"
file = "docs/wiki/PublicAPI.md docs/wiki/Getting-Started-Testnet.md platform/hosted/core/src/public_reads.rs"
symbol = "did_account_listing_unavailable lx_getBalances"
observed = "The account-listing boundary section of docs/wiki/PublicAPI.md and the lx_getBalances paragraph of docs/wiki/Getting-Started-Testnet.md described the did_account_listing_unavailable refusal as meaning only that account-listing evidence could not be decoded. The route now also returns it when the node reports a listing with no entries, because that answer carries no absence proof and so cannot be distinguished from an unavailable one, so the documented meaning had become narrower than the refusal the code emits. Both passages now name the empty listing as the second cause of the same refusal and state that every served entry carries state proof material. The code and error shape a client sees are unchanged; platform/hosted/gateway/openrpc.json, docs/wiki/PublicRpc.md and docs/wiki/HostedCore.md describe the route without naming a single cause for the refusal and needed no change."
assumption = "The two passages describe the behaviour this change alters, so they were brought in line in the same change rather than left to drift; no other page names a cause for the refusal."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.2.5905]
task = "6.2"
file = "platform/hosted/core/tests/boundary.rs tests/support/lxgb_metadata.rs src/modules/asset/lx_asset_registry.c"
symbol = "authenticated_receipt_wait_returns_on_commit_and_bounds_missing_receipts account_proof_export_preserves_exact_native_verified_bytes supervisor_reset_rebuilds_genesis_and_replays_once public_read_selectors_use_real_node_and_refuse_missing_evidence"
observed = "Re-running the gate on the revision rebased onto b01a0d6df reproduces observation 6.2.5902 unchanged: cargo test -p layerx-platform-core --test boundary exits 101 with none of the fourteen tests passing, because issuer_valid at src/modules/asset/lx_asset_registry.c:579-590 still requires the custody kind to equal the issuer kind and a non-empty custody reference for issuer kind 2, which the shared fixture record at tests/support/lxgb_metadata.rs:13 does not satisfy; the asset pause and unpause transitions merged in the meantime did not change that predicate. Evidence: qual-logs/boundary-r2-main-fixture.log. With the same fixture emitting the accepted record shape and nothing else changed, the same binary exits 101 with thirteen of the fourteen passing, including both tests this row owns, supervisor_reset_rebuilds_genesis_and_replays_once and public_read_selectors_use_real_node_and_refuse_missing_evidence; evidence qual-logs/boundary-r2-probe-fixture.log. The single failure there is authenticated_receipt_wait_returns_on_commit_and_bounds_missing_receipts, whose concurrent receipt reader panics at boundary.rs:56 with Transport(PeerShutdown) while performing the LNI handshake. That test passed in both earlier probe runs of the same binary, at 029f44dec and at b6c7f31e9, and in the run at b6c7f31e9 a different test failed instead: account_proof_export_preserves_exact_native_verified_bytes saw the account balance route answer 503 account_evidence_unavailable at boundary.rs:2862, and that test passes here. Neither test reads the DID listing nor starts the supervised cluster, so neither exercises either hunk on this branch; both are the harness losing a real daemon connection or a state proof's freshness while the host was running many compilations at once. The fourteen of fourteen result recorded in observation 6.2.5902 therefore describes one run at 029f44dec rather than a stable property: this harness starts real daemons with fixed waits and needs an unloaded host before a single run can be read as a verdict."
assumption = "The two intermittent failures are in tests outside this row and each passed on other runs of the same binary, so they are recorded rather than repaired, and no assertion, wait or timeout was changed to make a run green."
severity = "suspect"

[observation.6.2.5906]
task = "6.2"
file = "tests/support/lxgb_metadata.rs platform/hosted/core/tests/boundary.rs"
symbol = "lxgb_metadata::append rustfmt"
observed = "rustfmt --edition 2021 --check platform/hosted/core/tests/boundary.rs exits 1 and the entire diff it reports lies in tests/support/lxgb_metadata.rs, which boundary.rs:4-5 pulls in with a #[path] module so rustfmt formats it as part of the test target: the public_hex iterator chain at line 4 of the fixture exceeds the default width and rustfmt wants it broken across four lines. That file is byte-identical to origin/main's copy and rustfmt exits 1 on the copy extracted from origin/main as well, so the finding is not introduced here; platform/hosted/core/src/public_reads.rs formatted on its own exits 0. Evidence: qual-logs/rustfmt-r2.log, qual-logs/rustfmt-fixture-main-r2.log and qual-logs/rustfmt-public-reads-r2.log."
assumption = "The fixture belongs to the row that owns the genesis metadata record shape and is being rewritten there, so reformatting it from this branch would collide with that work; this branch changes no line of it."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.3.5700]
task = "6.3"
file = "include/layerx/lxp_batch_identity.h src/protocol/lxp_batch_identity.c agent/crates/layerx-wire/src/hash.rs"
symbol = "lxp_batch_identity_activity lxp_batch_identity_committed selected_execution_batch_id"
observed = "The batch execution identifier had two preimage forms and no single definition of either. The 80-byte activity form is previous_state_root(32) || activity_id(32) || global_sequence(8 big endian) || batch_number(8 big endian); the 88-byte committed form is previous_state_root(32) || activity_merkle_root(32) || first_sequence(8) || committed_last_sequence(8) || batch_number(8). Both are hashed under LXP_DOMAIN_CONTEXT_HASH, tag LXP/v1/context-hash. Six native sites open-coded one of the two layouts into a local buffer: cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_process.c at four call sites, cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_batch_wal.c lxp_daemon_batch_bind_prefix, cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_receipt_authority.c and cmd/layerx-guarantor/runtime.c. The wire crate reimplemented the same two layouts in agent/crates/layerx-wire/src/hash.rs and chose between them on a discriminator the daemon does not use: receipt module_id 9 with operation 3, that is the Programs deploy call, took the committed form and every other receipt took the activity form. The daemon does not discriminate that way. lxp_daemon_batch_bind_prefix binds the committed identifier to every execution in a batch and stamps every execution's activity_root with the committed activity tree, and the guarantor selects on exactly that stamp: cmd/layerx-guarantor/runtime.c:359 takes the batch-bound identity when expected->activity_root is non-zero and the scalar identity when it is zero. A receipt with a non-zero activity_root outside Programs deploy therefore carried the committed identifier while the wire crate recomputed the activity identifier, and the two disagreed. No specification document pins either preimage, so the row default applies and the daemon is authoritative: the layouts are now defined once in lxp_batch_identity_activity_preimage and lxp_batch_identity_committed_preimage, the four native producers and the guarantor call them, and layerx_wire::hash::selected_execution_batch_id selects on the receipt's own activity_root. The old module 9 operation 3 clause is retained inside the selection predicate rather than replaced, so the change is a pure widening of the committed path and no receipt that previously took the committed form now takes the activity form."
assumption = "The producer of the identifier defines it. Where no document pins the preimage, the daemon's binding is the specification and every other implementation must reproduce it. The receipt's own activity_root is the discriminator the daemon and the guarantor already agree on, so keying the wire crate's selection on it removes the disagreement at its source rather than adding a second discriminator."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.3.5701]
task = "6.3"
file = "agent/crates/layerx-agentd/src/protocol_evidence.rs platform/hosted/authority/src/lib.rs"
symbol = "receipt_execution_batch_id BatchIdentityEvidence"
observed = "platform/hosted/authority/src/lib.rs:344 and :346 branch on BatchIdentityEvidence, calling receipt_execution_batch_id for Historical and receipt_execution_batch_id_maintenance for OccupancyMaintenanceV2, because under occupancy the daemon publishes a maintenance receipt that consumes the final sequence and the committed range is therefore first_sequence..last_sequence-1. agent/crates/layerx-agentd/src/protocol_evidence.rs:349 calls receipt_execution_batch_id unconditionally with no maintained branch and no maintenance record in scope, so an agentd verification of evidence produced under occupancy maintenance computes the committed range as first_sequence..last_sequence and rejects the receipt with ReceiptEvidenceError::BatchIdentity. Keying the selector on the receipt's activity_root fixes the bound case for agentd as a side effect, since a bound receipt now reaches the committed form through the same call; the residual gap is the zero-activity_root receipt in a maintained batch, where the committed range must be narrowed by one and only the maintenance record proves that it should be. Closing it requires agentd to carry the maintenance record alongside the header, which is a change to the agentd evidence envelope and outside this row's owned paths."
assumption = "A verifier that cannot see whether a maintenance receipt was published cannot reconstruct the committed range, so the gap is in what the agentd envelope carries rather than in the identifier definition."
severity = "suspect"

[observation.6.2.5702]
task = "6.2"
file = "platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/lifecycle.rs platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/core_fixture.rs cmd/layerx-genesis/lxp_genesis_build_cli.c"
symbol = "local_gateway_lifecycle build_artifacts"
observed = "The gateway local lifecycle gate cannot reach any assertion on this revision. cargo test --manifest-path platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/Cargo.toml --test lifecycle local_gateway_lifecycle exits 101 with a panic at platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/core_fixture.rs:144 reporting that build/bin/layerx-genesis-build failed on [request.lxgb, signer.key, artifacts] with empty stderr. The cause is the LXGB metadata fixture already recorded in observation.6.1.5801: decoding the leftover request with the library directly shows the asset record at offset 399 length 154 refused by lx_asset_record_decode with LXP_ERR_NON_CANONICAL, so cmd/layerx-genesis/lxp_genesis_build_cli.c parse_request fails at line 203 and line 689 returns 1. The fee schedule in the same request decodes cleanly at version 2, so the asset record is the only defect. Two facts are added here. First, the failure reproduces with a genesis binary built from an unmodified tree and the batch identifier change set aside, and layerx-genesis-build links only cmd/layerx-genesis/main.c, lxp_genesis_build_cli.c, lxp_genesis_builder.c, liblayerx.a and the programs runtime, none of which this row changes, so the gate failure is independent of it. Second, the CLI writes nothing to stderr on the refusal, which is why a harness that shells out to it reports only a bare non-zero exit and the refused stage has to be recovered by decoding the request outside the tool. Consequence for this row: the batch identifier change is qualified by the native suite and the layerx-wire suite, and the live gateway lifecycle gate could not execute."
assumption = "A tool that exits non-zero without naming the refused stage or the result code turns every downstream harness failure into an undiagnosable bare exit. The fixture and the module disagree about how issuer_kind and custody_kind relate; both are left exactly as written and the repair belongs to whoever owns the fixture."
severity = "blocker"

[observation.6.2.5703]
task = "6.2"
file = "platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/Cargo.lock platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/Cargo.toml"
symbol = "layerx-program-sdk"
observed = "The first cargo invocation against platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/Cargo.toml on this revision reports Locking 1 package to latest compatible version, Adding layerx-program-sdk v0.1.0, and rewrites the checked-in lock file. The harness lock is therefore stale relative to the dependency graph on main and every run of the gateway local lifecycle gate leaves a modified lock behind in the working tree. The lock was restored to the revision's version here rather than committed, since it is outside this row's owned paths."
assumption = "A checked-in lock that the first build must repair means the lock was committed before the dependency was added; the repair is deterministic and belongs to whoever owns the harness."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.2.5704]
task = "6.2"
file = "platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/lifecycle.rs platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/verifier.rs tests/support/lxgb_metadata.py"
symbol = "local_gateway_lifecycle evidence_batch_id metadata"
observed = "The gateway local lifecycle gate was executed again after the batch identifier change was rebased onto a later main. cargo test --manifest-path platform/hosted/gateway/tests/local/Cargo.toml --test lifecycle local_gateway_lifecycle -- --exact --nocapture compiles the whole harness, including the verifier change that derives the identifier from the batch evidence class, and then exits 101 with the same panic at core_fixture.rs:144: build/bin/layerx-genesis-build refuses the generated request with an empty stderr. tests/support/lxgb_metadata.py still emits the version 3 asset record whose issuer and custody kinds disagree, which is the refusal recorded in observation.6.1.5801 and diagnosed in observation.6.2.5702, so the gate is blocked before any batch identifier code is reached: the panic happens while the fixture builds genesis, ahead of daemon start, receipt production and verification. The harness compiling clean is the part of this gate the change can reach, and it does; the lock rewrite of observation.6.2.5703 recurred on the first invocation and the lock was restored to the revision's version again."
assumption = "A gate that cannot construct its genesis fixture cannot qualify or disqualify anything downstream of it. The compile covers the changed harness code and the run reproduces an independently owned refusal, so the gate is recorded as blocked rather than failed against this change."
severity = "note"

[observation.2.2.7100]
task = "2.2"
file = "cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_lni.c"
symbol = "lxp_daemon_lni_simulate lni_principal lxp_authority_resolve_activity"
observed = "Supersedes observation 2.2.2100. lxp_daemon_lni_simulate no longer synthesizes its authority. It resolves the identity, refreshes it under governance, refuses an authority field that is not 32 bytes and then calls lxp_authority_resolve_activity with the kernel, the refreshed identity, the activity, the owner-key validity lxp_identity_key_valid reports for the batch timestamp and global sequence, the 300000 millisecond window the execution already declares and the same global sequence, which is the exact call cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_process.c makes at lines 1187, 2959 and 3440. The zero grant identifier, the UINT64_MAX per-activity, total and per-period ceilings and the direct lxp_authority_hash call are gone; the resolved grant carries the envelope lxp_authority_envelope_declare reads from the kernel's enabled module registrations and the authority hash commits to the grant identifier lxp_grant_id_compute derives. lni_principal gained the account_key parameter principal_authority already takes, so the account authority key is compared against the resolved grant key for an owner grant and the identity primary key otherwise, matching cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_process.c:1194. The batch timestamp used for key validity and resolution is owner->latest_sealed_timestamp, the same value the simulated execution already stamped into execution.batch_timestamp_ms, instead of the wall clock the file read before; the owner grant window comes from the activity timestamp bound, so the authority hash does not depend on which of the two a caller picks."
assumption = "The simulation must agree with the execution it predicts, so it resolves against the same batch timestamp the simulated execution carries rather than the wall clock. wall_clock_milliseconds keeps its other caller at cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_lni.c:1950. Evidence: tests/daemon/lxp_test_lni_account.c authority_resolution_matches_the_node, which builds the owner grant for the fixture activity, computes the authority hash over the derived grant identifier and over the zero identifier the previous synthesis used, and requires lxp_authority_resolve_activity to produce the first and not the second."
severity = "note"

[observation.2.2.7101]
task = "2.2"
file = "platform/emulator/core/emulator_core.c platform/emulator/core/emulator_core.h"
symbol = "owner_authority platform_emulator_resolve_authority lxp_authority_resolve_activity"
observed = "Supersedes observation 2.2.2101. owner_authority takes a grant, applies the governance module's persisted identity record through lxp_governance_identity_refresh as cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_process.c does before each of its three calls, and resolves through lxp_authority_resolve_activity against the emulator kernel, its refreshed identity store, its clock and its global sequence, with the timestamp window spelled once as PLATFORM_EMULATOR_TIMESTAMP_WINDOW_MS and shared with execution.maximum_timestamp_window. The zero grant identifier, the unconditional owner kind and the terminal lxp_authority_hash call are gone. The direct primary-key comparison the helper made is now the owner-key validity lxp_identity_key_valid reports, so a key the identity no longer holds falls through to lxp_authority_grant_lookup and is refused as a bad signature exactly as the node refuses it, and the state-commitment account binding compares the account authority key against the resolved grant key rather than the identity primary key. Actor and principal keep their previous values: lxp_authority_resolve writes the identity identifier into both, which is what lxp_did_id_derive produced before, and the non-programs branch still overwrites the principal with the payment account identifier, matching principal_authority in cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_process.c. Resolution now applies lxp_activity_check_timestamp_bound before execution rather than only inside lxp_kernel_prepare, against the same clock and the same window, so an activity the kernel would have refused is refused one step earlier with the same code."
assumption = "No emulator surface returns the authority a activity resolves to, so the parity proof needs an inspection entry: platform_emulator_resolve_authority decodes an activity, runs the same envelope, version, signature and owner_authority sequence platform_emulator_execute runs, and copies the resolved authority and the grant behind it into platform_emulator_authority without touching emulator state. Evidence: platform/emulator/tests/authority.rs, which recomputes the authority hash in Rust from the published LXP/v1/authority-hash domain tag over the kind, the returned grant identifier and the verified key, requires the emulator to return that value and not the hash over the zero identifier, and pins the owner grant to the declared envelope - module mask exactly the asset, governance and programs bits, never UINT64_MAX, ordinal range one to eleven and all three spend ceilings zero."
severity = "note"

[observation.2.2.7102]
task = "2.2"
file = "cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_lni.c"
symbol = "lni_principal program_admission_decode send_submit"
observed = "The two admission-time callers of lni_principal, the bridge credit check in program_admission_decode and the asset send and withdraw path in send_submit, pass activity->authority.bytes as the account key, so admission binds the account authority key to the key the activity presents while execution and simulation bind it to the key the resolved grant carries. The two agree for an owner grant, whose key is the presented key, and disagree for any grant kind whose key is a session or delegated key. Nothing writes a grant record today - lxp_authority_grant_lookup reads records tagged AUTHORITY_GRANT_RECORD_TAG out of governance module state and no encoder in the tree writes that tag - so the divergence is unreachable on this revision."
assumption = "Admission runs before a kernel execution exists and this row owns the simulation and emulator resolution sites, not the admission contract, so both call sites were left behaving exactly as they did rather than moved onto a resolution that would change which activities a node admits. The repair, when a grant writer lands, is to resolve at admission through the same entry and pass the resolved grant key."
severity = "suspect"

[observation.2.2.7103]
task = "2.2"
file = "Makefile tests/daemon/lxp_test_lni_account.c"
symbol = "test-daemon-lni-account lxp_daemon_lni_simulate"
observed = "No test binary links lxp_daemon_lni_simulate. The daemon node interface objects are built into build/bin/layerxd and the test-daemon-lni-account rule compiles tests/daemon/lxp_test_lni_account.c against the core archive and lxp_daemon_lni_account.c alone, so the rewired simulation path cannot be called in process and the conformance lock this row adds proves the resolution lxp_daemon_lni_simulate now performs rather than the function itself. tests/daemon/lxp_test_program_admission.c reaches the same code over a real daemon socket, but the receipt an LX_SIMULATE reply carries does not include the authority hash, so the value under test is not observable from that side either."
assumption = "Makefile is outside this row's owned paths, so no link line was changed and no new test target was added. The request to link the node interface objects into a daemon test binary is recorded in the lane NEEDS file. The authority hash is consensus visible through the execution binding and the programs authorization legs recorded at observation 2.2.2103, so a divergence would surface as a prepare and commit mismatch rather than silently."
severity = "note"

[observation.2.2.7104]
task = "2.2"
file = "agent/crates/layerx-wire/src/hash.rs src/state/lxp_identity.c"
symbol = "did_id lxp_did_id_derive"
observed = "layerx_wire::hash::did_id hashes the LXP/v1/did-id domain tag directly over the DID bytes, while lxp_did_id_derive at src/state/lxp_identity.c:46 hashes the same tag over a two byte big-endian DID length followed by the DID bytes. The two therefore produce different identifiers for every DID. The Rust entry has two callers, human/crates/layerx-intents/src/compile.rs:454 and human/crates/layerx-intents/src/disclosure.rs:521, which fold the digest into an intent encoding and a disclosure check; core identity, the authority grantor and grantee fields and every account name derive from the C rule."
assumption = "Recorded rather than repaired because layerx-wire and layerx-intents are outside this row's owned paths and changing the Rust digest changes intent encodings. platform/emulator/tests/authority.rs therefore recomputes the core identifier itself, from the tag, the big-endian length and the DID bytes, rather than calling layerx_wire::hash::did_id, and the emulator returns the same value for the grantor, the grantee, the actor and the principal of an owner grant."
severity = "suspect"

[observation.2.2.7105]
task = "2.2"
file = "platform/emulator/core/emulator_core.c src/protocol/lxp_genesis.c"
symbol = "register_modules lxp_genesis_module_plan_default lxp_genesis_module_plan_register lxp_genesis_module_plan_matches"
observed = "The emulator could not boot at all under the state commitment protocol version before this change, so no emulator test that selects it ran. register_modules registered exactly two modules for that version, programs at ABI 4 and asset at ABI 1, while lxp_genesis_module_plan_resolve derives three from the single module table in src/protocol/lxp_genesis.c:40-59 - programs, asset and governance, the last gated on the state commitment version since the genesis table was consolidated. lxp_genesis_materialize refuses a kernel whose registrations do not match the plan, so native_genesis returned LXP_ERR_NON_CANONICAL and platform_emulator_create_for_protocol returned a null handle. Evidence: a probe built from the unmodified origin/main copy of emulator_core.c against this worktree's core archive prints the plan as module 9 ABI 4, module 1 ABI 1, module 7 ABI 1 against a kernel holding only the first two, lxp_genesis_module_plan_matches answering LXP_ERR_UNKNOWN_MODULE, and the protocol 3 constructor answering NULL while protocol 2 succeeds. register_modules now asks lxp_genesis_module_plan_default for the same plan and registers it through lxp_genesis_module_plan_register, so the emulator cannot drift from the genesis table again; the legacy occupancy branch keeps its own seven module list unchanged. The declared authority envelope therefore covers the asset, governance and programs bits rather than asset and programs alone, which is the envelope a node running the same version declares."
assumption = "platform/emulator/core is inside this row's owned paths and the emulator could not be exercised at the version this row is about until the registration matched the plan, so the two line repair was made here rather than recorded and left. It is the smallest change that removes the divergence at its source: the emulator now reads the same table the daemon, the genesis builder and the guarantor read. Evidence that it restores the path: cargo test -p layerx-platform-emulator now runs and passes the two gateway tests that boot that version, lifecycle_routes_execute_and_replay_real_native_state_receipts and native_and_legacy_profiles_keep_snapshot_and_module_versions_separate, which could not previously get a handle."
severity = "note"

[observation.3.6.9300]
task = "3.6"
file = "agent/schema/agent-api/identity.kvx agent/crates/layerx-agent-api/src/operation_generated.rs agent/crates/layerx-agentd/src/tenant.rs agent/crates/layerx-mcp/src/server.rs"
symbol = "operation.faucet.claim Operation::FaucetClaim OperationClass::for_operation authorized_scopes TOOL_CATALOGUE tool_operation FAUCET_REQUEST"
observed = "Observation 3.6.4300 recorded that the daemon-routed catalogue could not carry faucet.request without an agentd Operation and a scope alias. The Operation enum is not hand-written: agent/crates/layerx-agent-api/src/operation_generated.rs carries the header that it is generated from the Agent API schema and must not be edited, and platform/sdk/generators derives it together with the Go, JVM, .NET, Swift, TypeScript and Python operation catalogues plus platform/sdk/conformance/operations.json and the platform/sdk/pipeline.kvx lock. The operation was therefore declared at its source, agent/schema/agent-api/identity.kvx:84-93, as type.FaucetGrant and operation.faucet.claim with required did and signer_public_key, a test_network_only network scope, the signer binding the funding boundary enforces and the idempotency binding it derives, and every generated artifact was rewritten by the repository generators rather than by hand. OperationClass::for_operation at agent/crates/layerx-agentd/src/tenant.rs:112 is an exhaustive match, so the generated variant forced the authorization decision at compile time: Operation::FaucetClaim is classed Write at tenant.rs:152 and authorized only by the any-of list at tenant.rs:183, write and write:faucet:claim. agent/crates/layerx-mcp/src/server.rs:52-58 now holds the single ToolDefinition, TOOL_CATALOGUE at server.rs:60-202 serves it as its twenty-first tool, tool_operation at server.rs:697-715 maps faucet.request onto the new Operation, and platform/cli/src/toolset.rs:4 and :38 consume the same constant instead of a second literal, so the two MCP surfaces cannot drift apart on the tool name, kind, scope, mutation or evidence."
assumption = "Attaching a faucet scope alias to an existing Operation was rejected: authorized_scopes returns an any-of list, so write:faucet:claim on Submit or Prepare would let a faucet-scoped session submit activities, and tenant.rs already pins that non-cross-authorization for the wait and grant aliases. Declaring the operation in the schema is the only construction that keeps the alias bound to exactly one operation, which agent/crates/layerx-agentd/src/tenant.rs:507-529 now asserts against every member of Operation::ALL."
severity = "note"

[observation.3.6.9301]
task = "3.6"
file = "agent/schema/agent-api/v1.kvx agent/crates/layerx-agent-api/build.rs agent/crates/layerx-agent-api/tests/schema.rs"
symbol = "schema.minor compatibility.history compatibility.feature ContractVersion"
observed = "The contract gains an operation while schema.minor stays 2, which departs from the only precedent the schema records. agent/schema/agent-api/v1.kvx:7 allows a release to add sections or keys within one major version, and the gate enforces exactly that and no more: compatibility_gate at agent/crates/layerx-agent-api/build.rs:88-121 fails on a changed or removed baseline key, skips schema.minor outright, and never inspects added sections, so operation.faucet.claim in identity.kvx passes with the minor untouched. The history beside it tells a different story: agent/schema/agent-api/v1.kvx:76-84 records 1_1 as the minor that added approval.kvx and 1_2 as the minor that added the Programs operation family, and v1.kvx:86-87 declares compatibility.feature.programs introduced_contract 1.2 so a negotiated peer can tell whether the family exists. The faucet operation carries neither a history entry nor a feature declaration, so a peer negotiated at 1.2 cannot tell from the version alone whether faucet.claim is available. Raising the minor is not a one-line change: the value is pinned by agent/crates/layerx-agent-api/src/generated.rs:68, which build.rs:373-377 byte-compares against the source it regenerates from v1.kvx, and asserted independently at agent/crates/layerx-agent-api/tests/schema.rs:104 and :122 and at agent/crates/layerx-sdk/tests/sdk.rs:91."
assumption = "The minor was left at 2 rather than bumped, because moving it means writing a compatibility.history.1_3 entry and a feature declaration for the faucet family and moving three pinned assertions, which is a contract-version decision wider than the tool that consumes it. Neither the schema nor any check in the tree requires the bump, and the daemon refuses an unknown operation name on its own, so nothing is weakened by leaving it; what is lost is the ability to discover the operation from the negotiated version."
severity = "suspect"

[observation.3.6.9302]
task = "3.6"
file = "platform/cli/src/toolset.rs platform/cli/src/install/mod.rs"
symbol = "SERVED required_scope gateway_scopes validate_issued_key"
observed = "The two MCP surfaces mean two different things by required_scope. On the daemon-routed surface it is authorization: agent/crates/layerx-mcp/src/server.rs filters the catalogue by the session scopes and every call re-authorizes through layerx-agentd, so write:faucet:claim decides whether the tool exists and whether it runs. On the gateway-direct surface it is a label: platform/cli/src/toolset.rs:123-128 only collects the strings for the install manifest and platform/cli/src/toolset.rs:145-153 echoes one into the tool descriptor, while the scopes actually provisioned on the gateway key are the fixed list at platform/cli/src/install/mod.rs:589-593, receipt:read alone in read-only mode and activity:write with receipt:read otherwise, which validate_issued_key at install/mod.rs:693-709 requires the gateway to have issued exactly. The faucet tool's scope was never in that list before this change and is not in it now; the gateway-direct claim is admitted by the stored identity session that platform/cli/src/toolset.rs:225-246 loads, not by the installation key. Sharing one definition moved the advertised label from faucet:claim to write:faucet:claim, and no other reference to the old string remained anywhere in the tree."
assumption = "One definition serving both surfaces is the row's default decision. It costs the gateway-direct surface nothing, because that surface never checked the label, and it makes the daemon-routed surface the one place where the string has to be right."
severity = "note"

[observation.3.6.9303]
task = "3.6"
file = "agent/crates/layerx-mcp/src/server.rs platform/hosted/gateway/src/rpc_faucet.rs platform/hosted/gateway/deployment.yaml platform/hosted/testnet/deployment.yaml"
symbol = "TOOL_CATALOGUE faucet.request request_funds faucet_not_configured"
observed = "The daemon-routed tool is authorized end to end, and the hosted wiring that observation 3.6.4301 recorded as absent has since landed on main: platform/hosted/gateway/deployment.yaml:86-87 sets LAYERX_GATEWAY_FAUCET_URL and LAYERX_GATEWAY_FAUCET_SERVICE_TOKEN_FILE and mounts Secret layerx-gateway-faucet-client at /run/layerx/faucet (gateway/deployment.yaml:115, 128), platform/hosted/testnet/deployment.yaml:147 hands the faucet the same Secret through LAYERX_FAUCET_SERVICE_CLAIM_TOKEN_FILE, the gateway egress rule at gateway/deployment.yaml:191-192 and the layerx-faucet-public-ingress rule at testnet/deployment.yaml:310-311 admit the edge on port 9443, and platform/hosted/tests/beta-cluster.sh:521 and :790 generate and apply the token. A session holding write:faucet:claim is offered faucet.request, layerx-agentd authorizes the call and the audit carries the attempt and its completion; the grant itself is the gateway method lx_requestFunds, whose request_funds at platform/hosted/gateway/src/rpc_faucet.rs:162-214 keys the reservation by principal, DID and signer key and answers every failure with a typed data.code: faucet_not_configured on a cluster that runs without those variables, session_required, signer_not_bound, faucet_rate_limit or faucet_unavailable otherwise. Each of those is an honest refusal from the funding boundary rather than a tool that refuses because it was advertised without authority, which is what 3.6.4300 warned about, and agent/crates/layerx-mcp/tests/faucet.rs drives a funded completion and a refusal through the real session control path."
assumption = "Advertising the tool through the daemon surface is correct because any refusal is typed and comes from the funding boundary. Whether a deployed cluster funds a claim through this path was not exercised here; that evidence belongs to the record that closes 3.6.4301."
severity = "note"

[observation.3.6.9304]
task = "3.6"
file = "docs/wiki/RunningAnAgent.md docs/wiki/HostedFaucet.md"
symbol = "TOOL_CATALOGUE SERVED path:line citations"
observed = "Observation 3.6.4304 recorded that nothing in the tree verifies wiki path:line citations. Adding one tool to each catalogue moved the ranges two pages cite and left one table short. docs/wiki/RunningAnAgent.md:27 says twenty tools in TOOL_CATALOGUE at agent/crates/layerx-mcp/src/server.rs:51-192, which is now twenty-one at server.rs:60-202; RunningAnAgent.md:362 and :569 cite the same old range; RunningAnAgent.md:573 cites tool_operation at server.rs:687-704, which is now server.rs:697-715, and the mapped-operation list at RunningAnAgent.md:570-573 lacks FaucetClaim; the layerx-mcp table at RunningAnAgent.md:546-567 lacks the faucet.request row that agent/crates/layerx-mcp/README.md carries. RunningAnAgent.md:26 and :540 cite SERVED at platform/cli/src/toolset.rs:23-45, which is now toolset.rs:23-39 because the faucet entry became a reference to the shared constant, and every toolset.rs line cited after it on RunningAnAgent.md:26, :540 and :542 (104, 104-116, 118-125, 219-224, 231-253) moved up six lines; docs/wiki/HostedFaucet.md:21 cites the faucet entry at toolset.rs:38-44, which is now the single line toolset.rs:38."
assumption = "Both pages lie outside this change's declared paths and are left to their owner rather than remapped here; the exact old and new ranges are recorded so the remap is arithmetic rather than a search."
severity = "note"

[observation.4.1.8800]
task = "4.1"
file = "platform/hosted/tests/beta-cluster.sh .github/workflows/publish-images.yml"
symbol = "publish_images preflight_disk LAYERX_BETA_IMAGE_MIN_FREE_GIB"
observed = "beta-cluster.sh publish-images now names the publisher mode on behalf of its caller instead of forwarding its arguments unchanged: check, dry-run, push, promote and self-test map to --check, --dry-run, --phase push, --phase promote and --self-test, an absent mode is refused with the list of modes and an unknown mode is refused by name, and the options after the mode are still forwarded to publish-images.sh unchanged. The release workflow now publishes through that callsite: the push step runs beta-cluster.sh publish-images push and the promote step runs beta-cluster.sh publish-images promote, so both phases name their mode where the release binding is selected. preflight_disk takes the bound, the variable that carries it and the purpose from its caller rather than reading MIN_FREE_GIB directly, and refuses a bound that is not a whole number of GiB; the cluster bring-up passes LAYERX_BETA_MIN_FREE_GIB, which still defaults to 24 GiB, and beta-cluster.sh images passes LAYERX_BETA_IMAGE_MIN_FREE_GIB, which defaults to LAYERX_BETA_MIN_FREE_GIB so no bound is lowered anywhere the operator has not asked for it. The publish job of the image workflow sets LAYERX_BETA_IMAGE_MIN_FREE_GIB to 12 for itself. Evidence: qual-logs/callsite-checks.log records all thirteen outcomes, including that a 999999 GiB image bound refuses the image build naming LAYERX_BETA_IMAGE_MIN_FREE_GIB, that the same value refuses the bring-up naming LAYERX_BETA_MIN_FREE_GIB even when the image bound is set to 12, that push refuses outside the gated workflow and promote refuses without a digest record, and that the publisher self-test passes through the callsite; qual-logs/self-test.log records the publisher self-test run directly."
assumption = "A publisher that can move the beta tag must be told its phase by the caller, so an absent mode is a refusal and not a default; a job that only builds the image set is a different disk workload from a local cluster bring-up, so it carries its own bound while the bring-up keeps the one it had."
severity = "note"

[observation.4.1.8801]
task = "4.1"
file = ".github/workflows/publish-images.yml platform/hosted/tests/beta-cluster.sh"
symbol = "LAYERX_BETA_IMAGE_MIN_FREE_GIB preflight_disk"
observed = "The 12 GiB bound the publish job names is derived from the storage a standard hosted ubuntu-24.04 runner is documented to provide, 14 GB or 13.0 GiB of SSD space, and not from a measurement of what the seventeen-image build consumes. The workflow has never run: it triggers on an sdk-v tag push or a workflow_dispatch and no such tag exists, so neither the build of the seventeen Dockerfiles on a hosted runner nor the free space it needs has ever been observed. The bound therefore refuses a runner that is plainly too small before anything is pushed, but it does not establish that the build fits; if the build exhausts the runner disk it will fail inside docker build, after the preflight has passed."
assumption = "A preflight bound is a refusal threshold and not a capacity proof. The measurement can only come from the first hosted run; if that run shows the image set needs more than the runner provides, the answer is a disk-reclaim step or a larger runner for the publish job, not a lower bound."
severity = "suspect"

[observation.4.1.8802]
task = "4.1"
file = ".github/workflows/publish-images.yml platform/hosted/tests/publish-images.sh platform/release/src/main.rs"
symbol = "manifest_images checked_image_publication registry_images IMAGE_KEYS"
observed = "The release manifest emitter reads a publication bundle the image workflow does not write. platform/release/src/main.rs:61 names the bundle release-image-<registry>; manifest_images at lines 1426 to 1460 requires one such bundle for every declared image registry; checked_image_publication at lines 1682 to 1722 requires its publication.txt to carry registry, version, distribution, the exact declared image list and a 40-hex revision; registry_images at lines 1734 to 1762 reads images.kvx as one section per image, keyed image.<name>, with the nine keys IMAGE_KEYS lists at lines 66 to 76, name, tag, digest, platform, signature, sbom, sbom_digest, attestation and attestation_digest, and refuses a declared image with no record. The publisher writes only build/beta-cluster/publication/publication.txt in the shape platform/hosted/tests/publish-images.sh write_publication_record defines, plus digests.txt, the SPDX SBOMs and the verification JSON, and the workflow retains that directory as the beta-image-publication artifact; nothing emits publication.txt in the release shape or images.kvx at all, so the release-verification job cannot emit an artifact manifest that carries images. This is the second item of the request lane L2 recorded in its NEEDS file. It is not fixed here: this row owns the inputs of the image workflow that select the publisher mode and the disk bound, and platform/hosted/tests/publish-images.sh, where the digests, signatures and SBOM digests that the bundle records are known, is outside its owned paths."
assumption = "The bundle must be written by the job that knows the published digests and the retained SBOM and attestation bytes, which is the publish or promote phase of the image workflow, and the emitter that hashes a retained file must run where those bytes are. Until it is written the manifest can only describe packages."
severity = "blocker"

[observation.4.1.8803]
task = "4.1"
file = ".github/workflows/publish-images.yml platform/hosted/tests/publish-images.sh"
symbol = "gate publish-images self-test"
observed = "No job runs the publisher self-test. The gate job checks the publisher and the shared image table with bash -n only, and does not syntax-check beta-cluster.sh although the publish job runs it, both to build the image set and now to select the publisher mode. The self-test cannot run in the gate job as it stands: mode_self_test reads a real local image inventory and its dry run generates an SPDX document per image, so it needs docker, syft and the seventeen built images, none of which the gate job has. It was run here against an inventory of seventeen real local images that satisfy every check the publisher makes but are not builds of the seventeen Dockerfiles, the same fixture shape lane L1 used, because building the real set needs far more disk than this host has free."
assumption = "A self-test that needs the built image set belongs in the publish job after the images are built, or in a job that builds them; wiring it there is a change to the workflow steps rather than to the inputs this row owns."
severity = "note"
[observation.6.11.7300]
task = "6.11"
file = "tests/test_genesis_manifest.c src/modules/asset/lx_asset_registry.c"
symbol = "lxp_kernel_module_for_activity LX_ASSET_WITHDRAW execute_withdrawal"
observed = "Resolves observation 6.11.2603. src/modules/asset/lx_asset_registry.c:11-16 advertises eleven Asset activity types including LX_ASSET_WITHDRAW, and the module dispatches that ordinal with a real state transition: module_decode admits ordinal 9 only with its 108-byte payload (line 322), module_validate builds the withdrawal context for it (lines 380-385) and module_execute_impl routes it to execute_withdrawal (lines 273-295, 419-420), which calls lx_asset_withdraw_request in src/modules/asset/lx_asset_custody.c:330. The native daemon admits it through asset_activity_supported at cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_process.c:570-588. The assertion at tests/test_genesis_manifest.c:196-204 predated that dispatch: it resolved seven of the advertised ordinals and required ordinal 9 to be unresolvable. It now requires each of the eleven advertised activity types to resolve to a registration whose module id is LXP_MODULE_ASSET and whose abi version is the one lx_asset_module_iface advertises, requires that registration to advertise exactly eleven types, and requires ordinal 0 and ordinal 12 to be refused, so an ordinal the module does not declare is still caught. The module is unchanged. Build and test were not run in this lane; make CC=gcc test-genesis-bootstrap is the owner's gate for this file."
assumption = "An advertised ordinal that the module decodes, validates and executes with a real state transition is the module contract, and the genesis test describes that contract rather than the earlier reserved state. The hosted submission list at platform/hosted/core/src/main.rs:714 and the version 2 fee schedule ordinals at src/protocol/lxp_fee.c:7-8 still exclude ordinal 9; that is public-admission and pricing policy rather than a module-table fact and is unchanged here."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.11.7301]
task = "6.11"
file = "cmd/layerx-module-registry/test_registry.py cmd/layerx-module-registry/main.c"
symbol = "expected module 1 ordinals"
observed = "Resolves observation 6.11.2602. cmd/layerx-module-registry/main.c:137-159 prints, for every module of lxp_genesis_module_plan_default, the low sixteen bits of each advertised activity type, so its Asset row is 1 through 11 because that is what src/modules/asset/lx_asset_registry.c:11-16 advertises. The expectation at cmd/layerx-module-registry/test_registry.py:24 still listed 1 through 9, written before LX_ASSET_WITHDRAW, LX_ASSET_MINT and LX_ASSET_BURN were advertised. It now lists 1 through 11; main.c is unchanged because its output is derived from the registered interface and is correct. The Programs, Governance and Bridge rows and the custody insertion index are unchanged. Build and test were not run in this lane; make CC=gcc test-module-registry is the owner's gate."
assumption = "The registry document describes what the node registers, so the expectation follows the advertised table, consistent with 6.11.2601 and with the decision recorded in 6.11.7300."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.11.7302]
task = "6.11"
file = "tests/fixtures/pay1-retired-genesis.manifest tests/fixtures/pay1-retired-issuance.lxs tests/test_genesis_builder_cli.c src/state/lxp_state_root.c"
symbol = "signed_snapshot_migration_fixture lxp_genesis_verify_signature universal_leaves genesis_fee_schedule_is_current"
observed = "Supersedes observation 6.11.2604 on its cause and on its key. tests/fixtures/pay1-retired-genesis.manifest is a protocol 3, network 77 manifest with one parameter, one guarantor, the three system accounts and six module values. Its companion tests/fixtures/pay1-retired-issuance.lxs records module 9 at abi 4 with ten activity types and module 1 at abi 1 with ten activity types, 1 through 8, 10 and 11 and no ordinal 9, so both fixtures were produced by a build whose Asset table did not yet advertise LX_ASSET_WITHDRAW. src/state/lxp_state_root.c:511-576 commits each registration's activity_type_count and, with Programs registered, every activity type into the state root, so advertising the eleventh type moved the root lxp_genesis_state_root recomputes for that manifest and lxp_genesis_verify_signature returns LXP_ERR_ROOT_MISMATCH at tests/test_genesis_builder_cli.c:205. The manifest also carries a 215-byte governance fee.schedule value while lxp_fee_params_decode (src/protocol/lxp_fee.c:64-66) accepts only LXP_FEE_PARAMS_V2_BYTES, 247, for version 2. Its signer public key is the Ed25519 public key of the 32-byte seed 0x22 that the fixture test writes at tests/test_genesis_builder_cli.c:187 and that tests/daemon/pay1.py:83 uses as the sequencer seed, so the manifest is reproducible from the repository: platform/hosted/node/bootstrap.sh invoked as tests/daemon/pay1.py:112-117 invokes it, with that sequencer seed, the 0x11 treasury seed, network 77 and metadata from tests/support/lxgb_metadata.py, writes genesis.manifest through cmd/layerx-genesis. The fixture bytes are untouched here because regeneration needs the built layerx-genesis-build and layerxd; the exact commands are in the pull request under Generated artifacts pending. tests/test_genesis_builder_cli.c gained genesis_fee_schedule_is_current, which requires exactly one governance value keyed fee.schedule, exactly LXP_FEE_PARAMS_V2_BYTES long and decoding as version 2 with LXP_ASSET_FEE_PRICE_COUNT prices, and requires the fixture's protocol version to be LXP_PROTOCOL_VERSION_STATE_COMMITMENT, so a regenerated manifest that carries a stale schedule or an older protocol is refused before the signature step. Build and test were not run in this lane."
assumption = "The retired snapshot stays valid without regeneration: snapshot_load compares only module id and abi version of each registration (src/state/lxp_snapshot.c:890-894), reads the recorded activity types (lines 903-907) and verifies the recorded roots against the candidate built from them, the migration registers programs_module_registration_v4 and lx_asset_module_iface in the same order (cmd/layerx-genesis/lxp_genesis_builder.c:313-318) and uses the genesis manifest only for its signature, signer key, network id and protocol version (lines 291-295, 312, 317, 337, 363), so regenerating the manifest alone is expected to let the migration path pass. That expectation is unverified until the owner runs make CC=gcc test-genesis-bootstrap against the regenerated fixture."
severity = "blocker"

[observation.6.11.7303]
task = "6.11"
file = "tests/fixtures/pay1-retired-issuance.lxs src/protocol/lxp_fee.c"
symbol = "fee.schedule LXP_FEE_PARAMS_V2_BYTES lxp_snapshot_load_retired_issuance"
observed = "The retired snapshot fixture carries a module 7 fee.schedule key-value of 215 bytes, the version 2 encoding before pause and unpause were priced, and cannot be regenerated by the current builder because tests/test_genesis_builder_cli.c:209-213 requires it to carry the retired issuance key scheme, as observation 6.1.8702 records. The migration path never decodes that value: snapshot_load copies module key-values raw (src/state/lxp_snapshot.c:909-929), lxp_state_root hashes them, and lxp_fee_params_decode has no caller under src/state, src/storage or cmd/layerx-genesis, so the fixture test is not affected. A daemon restoring the migrated checkpoint would decode it at the fee schedule stage of cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_process.c:5295 and refuse the 215-byte value. No length assertion was added for the snapshot because the fixture is a frozen historical artifact and the assertion would be permanently red."
assumption = "The retired snapshot exists to prove the rename migration and is not restored by any test, so its stale schedule is a documented limit of the fixture rather than a defect in the migration. A daemon-restore test of a migrated checkpoint would need a retired-scheme snapshot with a 247-byte schedule, which the current builder cannot emit."
severity = "suspect"

[observation.6.11.7304]
task = "6.11"
file = "docs/wiki/Modules.md docs/wiki/Assets.md docs/wiki/PublicRpc.md src/modules/asset/lx_asset_registry.c"
symbol = "asset ordinal 9"
observed = "docs/wiki/Modules.md:73 states that ordinal 9 is reserved for withdraw and is not defined on the Asset module, docs/wiki/Assets.md:48 lists ordinal 9 as reserved with no public payload, and docs/wiki/PublicRpc.md:151 lists the admitted native Asset ordinals without 9. src/modules/asset/lx_asset_registry.c:11-16 advertises LX_ASSET_WITHDRAW and lines 322, 380-385 and 419-420 decode, validate and execute it, and cmd/layerxd/lxp_daemon_process.c:570-588 admits it natively; only the hosted submission path at platform/hosted/core/src/main.rs:714 refuses it. The module description is therefore wrong about the module table and the native admission list, while the hosted-admission statement is accurate. These documents are outside the paths of the change that resolved 6.11.2602 through 6.11.2604, so they are unchanged here."
assumption = "Modules.md should describe ordinal 9 as advertised and dispatched by the module with a 108-byte payload, admitted natively and refused only by the hosted submission path, and Assets.md should carry the withdraw payload shape. The correction belongs to the documentation owner."
severity = "suspect"

[observation.6.11.6414]
task = "6.11"
file = "agent/crates/layerx-types/src/account.rs agent/crates/layerx-wire/src/hash.rs agent/crates/layerx-crypto/src/disclosure.rs platform/cli/src/account.rs platform/hosted/gateway/src/lib.rs"
symbol = "AccountId::parse account_id_for_protocol semantics create production_route"
observed = "Per-asset account names are absent from the closed account namespace and protocol-3 hash validator. The disclosure signer supports Asset SEND and RECEIVE but refuses new asset ordinals as UnsupportedActivity. Hosted CLI account creation submits only email and display_name to the Human account endpoint; it does not register the local signing DID and main account. The gateway route set lacks the specified lx_* RPC surface and typed wallet query/commitment contracts. Source excerpts are retained in qual-logs/pay3/integration-blockers.log."
assumption = "Wallet registration, per-asset sending, token mutation and commitment verification require the owning account, disclosure and gateway interfaces. CLI-local payload fixtures do not establish signing, execution, receipt verification or public testnet compatibility. No signer bypass, acknowledgement success, account namespace widening or invented RPC evidence schema is introduced."
severity = "blocker"

[observation.6.11.6416]
task = "6.11"
file = "platform/cli/src/wallet_encoding.rs platform/cli/tests/wallet_encoding.rs platform/cli/tests/fixtures/wallet-assets-v1.json"
symbol = "AssetOperation::encode asset_id canonical_asset_vectors"
observed = "CLI-local native asset register, account-open, grant-revoke, mint and burn encoders match retained big-endian fixtures, including a UTF-8 name, maximum supply cap and amounts above u64. Boundary and zero-amount refusal tests pass. cargo fmt --manifest-path platform/cli/Cargo.toml --check, cargo clippy --manifest-path platform/cli/Cargo.toml --all-targets -- -D warnings, cargo test --manifest-path platform/cli/Cargo.toml --test wallet_encoding and cargo test --manifest-path platform/cli/Cargo.toml each exit 0. Logs: qual-logs/pay3/fmt-qualified.log, clippy-qualified.log, wallet-encoding.log and test-private-profile.log, with sibling exit files and exact environment setup in commands.txt."
assumption = "Qualification uses worktree-local SQLite and real GNOME Keyring packages plus umask 077. Earlier attempts exposed missing sqlite3.h, missing gnome-keyring-daemon and writable temporary profile ancestors; these environment requirements were supplied without changing existing checks. This is payload qualification only: wallet commands, disclosed signing of new ordinals, RPC commitment verification and their quickstart remain incomplete pending the recorded shared interfaces. Cluster and image gates were not run; cg is unavailable."
severity = "note"

[observation.6.11.6418]
task = "6.11"
file = "agent/crates/layerx-crypto/src/disclosure.rs platform/hosted/gateway/openrpc.json platform/cli/src/wallet.rs"
symbol = "decode_send source_sequence payment_preflight"
observed = "The merged disclosure decoder requires Send payload sequence == activity.account_sequence. The required envelope sequence is identity.next_sequence while the Send payload uses source account next_sequence. The public RPC snapshot cb795071935210c39e14c77d1361dc5f27e9bea5 specifies lx_getSequence(account_id) as an account read and contains no identity sequence read, identity registration, asset metadata/listing, or DID history method. Existing emulator GET /v1/state exposes account sequences and a global sequence, not the actor identity sequence. Raw contracts: qual-logs/pay3/openrpc.json and qual-logs/pay3/rpc-source.rs."
assumption = "Send must retain separate sequences and complete disclosure. Writes remain refused before signing until an authenticated identity sequence interface and a compatible Send disclosure decoder exist. No account or global sequence may substitute for the identity sequence."
severity = "blocker"

